Accumulating an estimated rapid increase in coronavirus disease (COVID-19) in the European Union or the European Economic Area and the United Kingdom from 1 January to 15 March 2020
The cumulative incidence of coronavirus (COVID-19) cases demonstrate similar trends in the countries of the European Union or the European Economic Area and the United Kingdom, which confirms that, although at different stages depending on the country, the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing rapidly in all countries.
Based on Italy &apos; s experience, countries, hospitals and intensive care units need to improve their preparation for a wave of patients with COVID-19 that will require medical care and, in particular, intensive care.
On December 31, 2019, a conglomerate of cases of unknown etiology pneumonia was reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, China's Center for Disease Control and Prevention reported that the causative agent was a new corona-trudigger that is now known as coronavirus 2 of Severe Accrust Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, illness caused by SARS-CoV-2 infection has been dubbed coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
The evidence to date indicates that 80 per cent of people with COVID-19 have a mild illness, i.e. a respiratory tract tract infection with or without pneumonia, and that the majority are recovering.
In the 14 per cent of cases, COVID-19 evolves towards a more serious illness that requires hospitalization, while the remaining 6 per cent of the cases develop a critical disease that requires intensive care.
Mortgage in patients hospitalized by COVID-19 is 4 per cent.
In this study, we examined trends in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in each country of the European Union or the European Economic Area (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom (UK) and compare them with those of Hubei Province, China.
We also compare the current number of COVID-19 cases in the EU/EEA countries and the UK with that of Italy from 31 January to 15 March 2020.
COVID-19 cases in EU/EEE countries and the UK
After China, COVID-19 continued to propagate geographically and the dynasty of the COVID-19 pandemic in the rest of the world currently follows that of this country.
On March 11, 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
In the 5 March edition of Eurosurveillance 2020, Spiteri et al. reported on the first confirmed cases of COVID-19 in Europe in accordance with the WHO case definition.
In the EU/EEA, the first three confirmed cases were recorded in France on 24 January 2020, in people who had returned from Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
As at 15 March 2020, COVID-19 cases had been detected in the 30 EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom (United Kingdom (RUs), according to 3 31 December 2019 and even that date, there had been 39,768 cases and 1727 deaths, of which 17 750 cases and 1441 deaths had occurred in Italy alone.
Obtaining the accumulated quantity and cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases
At the European Centre for Disease Control and the Prevention (ECDC), COVID-19 case counts reported in each country of the world, obtained only from official sources, such as the Ministry of Health, national and regional health authorities and WHO, are updated every day at 8 a.m.
These data were used to look at COVID-19 trends in the EU/EEE and the UK and to compare them with that of Italy.
As an indicator of the prevalence of the active cases of COVID-19, we calculated the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 truncated to 14 days; in this way, we took into account the natural course of COVID-19, in each EU/EEA country and the UK, during the period from 1 January to 15 March 2020.
We also present the cumulative number of cases reported in each country as at 15 March 2020 at 8:00 a.m., compared to that of Italy for the period from 31 January to 15 March 2020.
COVID-19 trends in EU/EEA countries and the UK
Trends in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases truncated at 14-day in EU/EEE countries and the UK, in general, followed that of Hubei Province (China) (Figure 1).
In the EU/EEA and the UK in general, the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 began to increase around 21 February and then increased considerably from 28 February 2020 (supplementary material).
This was mainly due to the rapid increase in the number of informed cases in Italy, but all other EU/EEA and RU countries exhibited upward trends in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 (supplementary material).
Figure 2 shows the cumulative amount of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the UK, compared to Italy for the period from 31 January to 15 March 2020.
It points out that, as at 11:00 a.m. as at 11 a.m., 15 other EU/EEE countries and the UK had already registered a total number of cases comparable to that of Italy only 3 weeks earlier or less.
Our results indicate that the number of reported COVID-19 cases is increasing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the UK.
The trends observed in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 suggest that the pandemic is progressing at a similar pace in all countries.
This is despite the different stages in which the countries find themselves, the variations in national public health responses and possibly the different case definitions in the countries and the different protocols for selecting the patients to be tested to confirm COVID-19, including delayed tests.
At the beginning of March 2020, the doctors from the affected regions of Italy described a situation in which 10 per cent of COVID-19 patients required intensive care, and the media reported that hospitals and intensive care units in these regions had already reached their maximum capacity.
COVID-19 case admissions data are currently available at EU/EEA level for only 6 per cent and 1 per per cent of cases respectively (the data are not shown).
However, they should be systematically collected to complement the current surveillance data that focus on the number of reported cases and the number of deaths.
A study carried out in 2010-11 showed a great variation in the availability of intensive care beds and mid-term care in Europe, ranging from 29,2 in Germany to 4.2 beds per 100 000 inhabitants in Portugal.
This means that countries can have more or less resources than Italy (12.5 intensive care beds and intermediate care every 100 000 inhabitants in 2010-11).
Model scenarios regarding the saturation of medical care capacity, with calculations for each EU/EEE country and the UK of the prevalence of COVID-19 hospitalization cases associated with a risk .90 per per cent of overcoming the capacity of intensive care beds, are provided in the sixth update of ECDC &apos; s rapid risk assessment on COVID-19.
Since case clusters have so far been formed in certain regions of EU/EEA countries and the UK, and hospitals and intensive care units normally serve a defined regional catchment population, information on intensive care cases and beds should preferably be made available at the Nomenclature of Statistical Territorial Units 2 (NUTS-2).
Italy &apos; s experience and current trends in other countries show that the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the UK.
Countries, hospitals and intensive care units must therefore be prepared for a sustained SARS-CoV-2 Community Transmission scenario and an increase in the number of COVID-19 patients who require medical attention and, in particular, intensive care, such as that occurring in the affected regions of Italy.
As indicated in ECDC's rapid risk assessment, it is essential to take a rapid, proactive and comprehensive approach to delaying the spread of SARS-CoV-2, with a shift from a containment approach to a mitigation approach, as the anticipated rapid increase in the number of cases may not provide sufficient time for decision-makers and hospitals to comprehend, accept and adapt their response accordingly if it is not implemented in advance.
The rapid risk assessment also lists the public health measures needed to mitigate the impact of the pandemic.
There's a brief period of opportunity during which countries have the opportunity to deepen their control efforts to curb the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce pressure on the health system.
If this is not done, the health systems of other EU/EEA countries are likely to face a wave of patients requiring intensive care in the next few days and weeks.
The fission of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19), Caused by Coronavirus 2 of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) (SARS-CoV-2), has so far resulted in the deaths of 3000 people and infected more than 80,000 in China and elsewhere around the world, creating a catastrophe for humans.
Similar to its counterpart virus, SARS-CoV, which triggered SARS in thousands of people in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 could also be transmitted from the bat and causes similar symptoms using a similar mechanism.
COVID-19, however, has lower severity and mortality than SARS, but is much more transmissible and affects older people more than young people and more men than women.
In response to the growing number of publications on the emerging disease, the purpose of this article is to provide a timely and comprehensive analysis of the topic of research, which develops rapidly.
We will deal with the basic aspects of epidemiology, typology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis and the prevention of the disease.
Although many questions do not yet have an answer, we hope that this analysis will help to understand and eradicate this threatening disease.
The Spring Feast of January 25, 2020 has become an unheard-heard-hensible memory for all Chinese, who were urged to stay at home throughout the festivity and for several more weeks due to the outbreak of a new viral disease.
The virus has almost the same name as the coronavirus (CoV) that caused an outbreak of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) in 2003; therefore, on February 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) called it SARS-CoV-2, and the associated disease was dubbed coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).
The Epidemic began in Wuhan, China and spread rapidly throughout the country and to nearly 50 other countries around the world.
As of 2 March 2020, the virus has caused more than 80,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19, with more than 40,000 patients discharged and more than 3,000 patients killed.
The WHO warns that COVID-19 is public enemy number 1 and is potentially more powerful than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), for less than two months, more than 200 scientific articles have been published on COVID-19, including its virology, epidemiology, etiology, diagnosis and treatment, from the first report of January 7, 2020, which determined the sequencing of the virus isolated from multiple patients.
The objective of this analysis is to summarize the progress made in research on this rapidly developing new area.
Where possible, we will try to compare COVID-19 with SARS and another disease caused by a CoV, Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS), an outbreak in 2012.
We'll also look at what's known so far about the prevention and prognosis of the disease, as well as some crucial questions that have not yet been answered.
Coronaviruses have traditionally been considered non-lethal pathogens for humans, which mainly cause about 15 per cent of common cooler 4.
In this century, however, we've found ourselves twice with highly pathogenic coronaviruses in humans, i.e. SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which originally caused an outbreak in China in 2003, and in Saudi Arabia in 2012, respectively, and soon spread to many other countries with horrific morbidities and mortality.
Therefore, COVID-19's current fission shoot is the third by coronavirus that is recorded in the history of humanity.
As shown in fig. 1,1, conglomerates of pneumonia of unfamiliar origin were first reported on December 31, 2019 from Wuhan to China's National Health Commission.
Seven days later, the coronavirus sequential was released.
On January 15, 2020, the first deadly case was recorded in Wuhan.
In the meantime, the epidemic spread rapidly to cities, provinces and neighboring countries.
On January 20, health-care provider infection was reported, which suggested transmission between humans was possible.
On January 23, the city of Wuhan was quarantined and all public transport was suspended.
On January 24, the first clinical study on the disease reported that, of the 41 patients with confirmed cases, only 21 had had direct contact with Wuhan's seafood market that was being considered the source site of infection of an unnamed animal fountain.
On January 30, the WHO declared a global health emergency to the outbreak.
At the time of this report, the disease has already spread throughout China and nearly 50 more countries around the world (fig. 2).
Since the situation evolves rapidly, the final dimension and severity of the outbreak cannot yet be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a multicenter study of 8866 patients, including 4021 patients with Confirmed SOVID-19, presented a more up-to-date illustration of the epidemic described below (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/s/UlBi-HX-rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
The SARS-CoV-2 infected people of all ages, but mainly people ages 30 to 65.
Almost half (47.7 per cent) of those infected were more than 50 years of age, very few were less than 20 and only 14 infected people were under the age of 10.
The SARS-CoV-2 infected men (0,31/100 000) more than women (0,27/100 000).
COVID-19 expanded into conglomerates mostly in Hubei and its environs.
On an average of 5 days (from 2 to 9) between the onset and diagnosis of COVID-19.
The average incubation period was 4.8 days (from 3,0 to 7,2).
The average time from onset to death was 9.5 days (between 4.8 and 13).
The basic reproductive number (R0) was 3.77 (IC 95 per cent: 3.51-4.05) and Adjusted R0 was 2,23-4.82.
The number of infected people increased exponentially before January 23, 2020, which coincides with the era of mass mobilization before the Spring Feast in China.
Mortality in patients with confirmed cases was 1.44 per cent (CI 95 per cent: 1,10-1.86 per cent), and the adhestriated mortality of all patients was 3.06 per cent (CI 95 per cent: 2,024.59 per cent).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 were sex (masculine), age (60) and severe pneumonia.
Coronaviruses are a subfamily of large and wrapped viruses that contain a single-sense string RNA.
They can be divided into four genres, i.e. alpha, beta, range and deltas, of which alphacoronaviruses and deltacoronaviruses are those that infect humans.
Spike glycoprotein (S) of wrapping binds to its cellular sensations, the angiotensin 2 converting enzyme (ACE2) and peptideid dipeptidyl 4 (DPP4) for SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, respectively, and the membrane melt melt melt occurs.
Visual RNA genome is released to the cytoplasm; after replication of the viral genome, genomic RNA, along with wrapping glycoproteins and nucleocápside proteins, forms gallbladders containing viriones, which are then merged with the plasma membrane to release the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 genomic sequencing was advertised January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 was undiscovered a new type of beta-coronavirus with a genetic identity of more than 99.98 per cent in 10 sequenced samples taken from the original outbreak site: Wuhan's Huanan seafood market.
The SARS-CoV-2 is genetically closer to SARS-CoV than MERS-CoV.
Using a transmission electron microscope, SARS-CoV-2 particles were found in ultra thin sections of the human respiratory epithelium.
Human ACE2 was found to be an SARS-CoV-2 receiver and SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 Prote Prote Prote Prote Protein Joins to Human ACE2 weaker than SARS-CoV, congruent with the fact that SARS-CoV-2 Causes a less severe infection in patients than SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form a new short protein encoded by orf3b and a secret protein encoded by orph8.
SARS-CoV-2 orf3b could have a function in viral pathogenicity and inhibit IFN expression; however, orf8 does not contain any known domain or functional motif.
On February 18, 2020, Zhou, et al., unveiled the electronic cryomicroscopy structure of human ACE2 of complete longitude in resolution 2.9 , in complex with the amino acid conveyor B0AT1.
They discovered that the complex, which had open and enclosed conformations, was assembled as a dimer, and that the ACE2-B0AT1 complex can link two S proteins, which provides evidence for coronavirus recognition and infection.
B0AT1 could become a therapeutic target for the study of drugs to suppress SARS-CoV-2 infection.
The origin and the intermediate guest
The SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV are known to have originated in bats and were transmitted to the humans through cvests and camels, respectively.
In an SARS-CoV-2 phylogenetic comparison with other coronaviruses, the bat was considered to be the native host of SARS-CoV-2, as the new virus is identical in 96 per cent to two SARS-like coronaviruses of bats called the bat-SL-CoVZX and bat-SL-CoVZX21.
However, it is still not known which mid-to-trad host helped the virus cross the species barrier to infect humans, and the transmission route remains to be clarified.
Ji, et al., proposed snakes as carriers of the bat virus to humans, which involved counterpart recombination within protein S.
According according to a study, researchers from Guangzhou, China, suggested that pangolines (long snout mammals that fed ants and are usually used in traditional Chinese medicine) are a possible intermediate SARS-CoV-2 host based on a 99 % genetic homology in a coronavirus discovered in pangolines and SARS-CoV-2.
However, a difference of 1 per cent propagated by all two genomes remains a great difference; therefore, conclusive results are expected to provide concrete evidence (fig. 33).
SARS-CoV-2 physics are still largely unknown.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can survive in vitro for 48 hours in a dry environment and for up to 5 days to less than 20 degrees Celsius with a humidity 40-50 per cent.
The SARS-CoV-2 could own similar properties.
SARS-CoV-2 it has been reported sensitive to ultra-violet rays and heat at 56 degrees Celsius for 30 minutes; ether, ethanol at 75 per chlorine disinfectant, perchloric acid,  Chloroforming and other fat solvents, but not chlorhexidine, are effective in deactivating the virus.
The entire human population in general lacks SARS-CoV-2 immunity and is therefore susceptible to the new coronavirus.
So far, no detailed study has been announced on the Immune Response to the SARS-CoV-2.
As a result, we can only rely on previous studies with other coronaviruses, especially SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (fig. 4).4).
In general, after the virus invades the host, it is first renowned by the in-innate immune system of the host through Pattern Receptors (PRRs), including Type C lectin receivereceices, Toll-type (TLR), NOD-type receptors (NNLR) and RIG-I (RLR) receiverectors.
Through different pathways, the virus induces the expression of inflammatory factors, the maturation of dendritic cells, and the synthesis of I-type interferons (IFNs) that limit the propagation of the virus and accelerate the macro-phagic phagocytosis of viral antigens.
SARS-CoV's promo N, however, can help the virus escape immune responses.
Soon the adaptive immune response adds to the fight against the virus.
T lymphocytes, including CD4 and CD8 T cells, play an important role in the defense.
CD4 T-cells steep B-cells to produce virus-specific antiquities, and CD8 T-cells directly kill cells infected with the virus.
Cosociative T-cells produce pro-inflammatory cytoocytes to help defense cells.
Coronaviruses, however, can inhibit the functions of T-cells by inducing Apoptosis of T-cells.
Humoral Immunity, which includes accessories such as C3a and C5a and antibodies, is also essential to combat viral infection.
For example, isolated antiquities of a recovering patient neutralized MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, an exaggerated reaction of the Immune system locally generates a large number of free radicals that can cause serious damage to the lungs and other organs, and in the worst-case, multi-ourinic failure and even death.
SARS-CoV-2 infection, characterized by conglomerate, most likely affects older people with comorbidities and pregnant women.
It is common for people who are exposed to a large number of viruses or whose immune functions are compromised are more likely to become more likely to become infected than others.
SARS-CoV-2 average incubation period is 1 to 14 days, mostly between 3 and 7 days, according to a study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan.
However, a study of 1099 cases shows that the incubation period was 3 days on average and ranged from 0 to 24 days.
A more recent study, as described above, shows that the incubation period was 4.8 days (from 3.0 to 7,2) based on the demography of 8866 cases.
It is very important for health authorities to adapt effective quarantine time according to the most accurate incubation period; this is how asymptomatic infected people are expected to transmit the virus to others.
As a standard practice, people exposed to the virus or infected should normally be quarantined for 14 days.
Should the quarantine be extended to 24 days?
The fever is usually the main and initial symptom of COVID-19, which may be accompanied by any other symptoms or by other symptoms such as cough, dyspnea, muscle pain, dizzy, headache, sore throat, rinorrhea, chest pain, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting.
Some patients had dyspnea or hypoxemia a week after the onset of the disease.
In severe cases, patients evolved rapidly to developing acute respiratory syndrome, septic shock, metabolic acidosis and coagulopathy.
Patients with fever or respiratory symptoms and acute fever, even without lung anomalies in imaging studies, should be tested on the virus for an early diagnosis.
A population study at the end of December of 2019 indicated that the percentages of the symptoms were 98 per cent for the fever, 76 per cent for dry cough, 55 per cent for dyspnea and 3 per cent for diarrhea; 8 per cent of patients required respiratory assistance.
Similar findings were reported in two recent studies of a family conglomerate and conglomerate caused by an asymptomatic transmission.
Comparably, a 2012 demographic study showed that MERS-CoV patients also had a fever (98 per cent), dry cough (47 per cent) and dyspnea (55 per cent) as major symptoms.
However, 80 per cent of them required respiratory assistance, much more than patients with CIOVID-19 and in line with MERS's highest lethality compared to COVID-19.
Diarrhea (Raphae (26 %) and sore throat (21 per cent) were also observed in patients with MERS.
In patients with SARS, fever (99 per cent-100 per cent), dry cough (29 per cent-75 per cent), dyspnea (40 per cent-42 per cent), diarrhea (20 per cent-25 per cent) and threshing sorrow (13 per cent-25 per cent) were the main symptoms and respiratory assistance was required in approximately 14 per cent to 20 per cent of patients.
As of February 14, COVID-19 mortality was 2 per cent when confirmed cases reached 66 576 worldwide.
Similarly, SARS Mortgage for November 2002 was 10 per cent in 8096 confirmed cases.
For MERS, according to a June 2012 population study, the mortality rate was 37 per cent in 2494 confirmed cases.
In an earlier study, it was reported that SARS-CoV-2 R0 was high and rose to 6.47 with a 95 per cent confidence interval (CI) from 5.71 to 7.23, while SARS-CoV R0 only ranged from 2 to 4.
A SARS-CoV-2 comparison to MERS-CoV and SARA-CoV in terms of symptom, mortality and R0 is presented in Table 1.1.
Previous figures suggest SARS-CoV-2 has a greater propagation capability than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but its lethality is lower than the latter two.
It is therefore much more difficult to control the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
Conglomerate appearances usually occur within the same family or from the same reunion or vehicle, such as a cruise ship.
Patients usually have a history of travel or residency in Wuhan and other affected areas or contact with infected persons or patients in the two weeks prior to on-appearance.
However, it has been reported that people can carry the virus without symptoms for more more than two weeks and that patients discharged from the hospital can re-actuate the virus, an alarm signal that quarantine time should be increased.
Patients have a normal or reduced amount of white blood cells in peripheral blood (especially lymphocytes) at the early stage.
For example, linflopenia was observed with a white blood cell count of 4.109/l, including a lymphocyte count of .109/l, and elevated levels of aspartato aminotransferase in 1099 patients with COVID-19.
Heath and myoglobin levels of liver and myoglobin enzymes were elevated in the blood of some patients, and reactive C-ediate protein and erythrocyte sediment were elevated in the blood of most patients.
In patients with serious cases, dimer-D level, a product of fibrin degradation present in the blood, was elevated, and the lymphocyte count was progressively reduced.
An anomalies are detected in thoracic X-rays of the majority of patients with COVID-19, characterized by shades or opacities in radical smerifying glass in the lungs.
Patients often develop an atypical pneumonia, an acute lung lesion and acute respiratory distress syndrome (RASD).
When SDRA occurs, uncontrolled inflammation, fluid build-up and progressive fibrosis severely affect gaseous exchange.
Dysfunction of Type I and Type II pneumocytes decreases the surfactant level and increases surface tension, which reduces the ability of the lungs to expand and increases the risk of lung collapsation.
As a result, the worst findings in thoracic X-rays are often in parallel with the maximum severity of the disease.
On February 18, 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 demonstrated the peeling of pneumocytes, the formation of a hialine membrane and infiltration of lymphocytes into the interstitium, and multinucleated syncytial cells in the lungs of a patient who died from the disease, which coincides with the pathology of a viral infection and SDRA, and similar to patients with SARS and MERS.
SARS-CoV-2 RNA detection using a reverse transcriptase polymerase (RT-PCR) chain reaction was used as the main criterion for the diagnosis of COVID-19.
However, due to the high rate of fake negatives, which could accelerate the epidemic, clinical manifestations for diagnosis (which were no longer based solely on RT-PCR) began to be used in China on February 13, 2020.
A similar situation also occurred with the SARS diagnosis.
Therefore, a combination of history of disease, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests and radiological findings is essential and imperative for an effective diagnosis.
On February 14, 2020, the Feng Zhang group described a protocol for the use of the SHERLOCK-based SHERLOCK technique based on CRISPR to detect SARS-CoV-2 SARS-CoV-2 Syndrome RNA fragments between 20 x 10-18 mol/l and 200 x 10-18 mol/l (10-100 copies per sample microliter) using a graduate rod in less than an hour that does not require complex instruments.
Hopefully, the new technique can drastically increase sensitivity and convenience if verified in clinical samples.
Due to the inexperience with the new coronavirus, physicians can mostly provide supportive care for patients with COVID-19, while at the same time testing with a variety of therapies that have previously been used or proposed for the treatment of other coronaviruses, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, and other viral diseases (Table 2).
These therapies include treatments currently in use or with antiviral drugs, immunosuppressants, steroid steroids, plasma from recovered patients, Chinese medicine and psychological support.
It was even proposed to use the plasma of recovered patients for the treatment.
Pharmaceutical companies are racing to see who first develops anti-imboys and virus vaccines.
The SARS-CoV-2 mainly attacks the lungs at the beginning and probably also attacks, to a lesser degree, other organs that express ACE2, such as those of the digestive system and kidneys.
Respiratory dysfunction and inconviction, however, are the greatest threat to patients and the leading cause of death.
As a result, respiratory care is critical for relieving symptoms and saving lives, including general oxygen therapy, high-flow oxygen, non-Invasive ventilation and invasive mechanical ventilation according to the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory sympathises need extracorporeal membrane oxygenation assistance (OMEC), a modified cardiopulmonary bypass techniculation technique that is used for the treatment of life-threatening cardiac or respiratory failure.
Furthermore, the maintenance of electrolytic balance, the prevention and treatment of secondary infections and septic shock, and the protection of the functions of the vital organs are also essential in SARS-CoV-2 patients.
cycycycling storm is known to be the result of an exaggerated immune system backlash in patients with SARS and MERS.
cytokine storm is a systemic inflammatory-response form characterized by the release of a series of cytokines, which include TNF, IL-1, IL-1, IL-1, IL-6, IFN, IFN and MCP-1.
These cytokines induce immune cells to unleash an enormous number of free radicals that are the main cause of SDRA and multi-organ failure.
Immunosuppression is essential for the treatment of cytokine thrunder, especially in severe patients.
corticosteroids and tocilizumab have been used, an IL6 anticlonal anticlonal anti-ceptor body, to treat cytokine storm.
Other immunosuppressive treatments for cytokine thrhalf are modulation of T-cell-directed immune response; blocking IFN, IL-1 and TNF; JAK inhibition; blinatumomab; a cytokine signalling supresor 4 cytokine signage suppressor and HDAC inhibitors.
Steroids, such as immunosuppressants, were widely used in SARS treatment to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
High-dose steroids, however, were not beneficial for serious lung lesions in patients with SARS and COVID-19.
On the other hand, they can cause serious side effects, in particular osteonecrosis, which significantly affects the prognosis.
Prudential use of corticosteroids at low to moderate doses, however, has been recommended over a short period for patients with COVID-19 in critical condition.
At the time of this analysis, the effectiveness of any antiviral therapy has not been confirmed.
However, the effectiveness of remdesivir, a nucleotydic analyn, administered intravenously in an U.S. patient with amOVID-19, has been proven.
Remdesivir is a new anti-viral drug originally developed by Gilead for the treatment of diseases caused by Ebola and Marburg Visruses.
Later, the remdesivir also demonstrated the potential for inhibiting other monocatenary RNA viruses, such as MERS and SARS viruses.
Based on these, Gilead has provided the compound to China to conduct a pair of tests on people infected with SARS-CoV-2, and there is great expectation for the results.
In addition, baricitinib, interferon alfa, lopinavir/ritonavir and ribavirin have been suggested as possible therapies for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, liver damage and other adverse reactions may occur after combination therapy with lopinavir/ritonavir.
The interaction of these treatments with other drugs used in patients should be carefully monitored.
Plasma of recovered patients and anti-body generation
Seizing blood from patients who recovered from a contagious disease to treat other patients who contracted the same disease or to protect healthy people so they don't contract the disease isn't new.
In fact, the recovered patients usually have a relatively high level of antibodies against the pathogen in the blood.
Antibodies are an immunoglobulin (Ig) produced by B lymphocytes to combat pathogens and other foreign objects, and recognize certain molecules in the pathogens and directly neutralize them.
On this basis, plasma was extracted from the blood of a group of patients who were recovering from COVID-19 and 10 seriously ill patients were injected.
Their symptoms improved in 24 hours, accompanied by a reduction in inflammation and viral loads and better saturation of oxygen in the blood.
However, it is necessary to verify and clarify this in order to propose the method for its large-scale use until specific therapies are developed.
In addition, given the therapeutic effects, some disadvantages associated with plasma should be carefully considered.
For example, antibodies can over-strengthen the immune response and cause cytokine release syndrome, possibly leading to fatal toxicity.
The concentration of antibodies in the blood is usually low, and the demand for plasma is high to treat patients in critical condition.
It is difficult to develop and produce specific antibodies quickly enough to combat a global pandemic.
Therefore, it is more important and practical to insolulate the B cells of recovered patients and to identify the genetic codes that en encode effective antibodies or seek effective antibodies against essential virus proteins.
In this way, we can easily increase the production of the antibodies.
MCT has been used for thousands of years to treat a variety of diseases in China.
Its effects, however, rely heavily on a combination of several components in a formula that varies according to the diagnosis of a disease according to MCT theories.
Most effective constituents are still unscathed or imprecise, as it is difficult to extract and verify such components or their optimal combinations.
At present, due to the lack of an effective and specific therapy for COVID-19, MCT has become one of the main alternative treatments for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or for those who have recovered from serious stages.
For example, Shu Feng Jie Du capsules and Lian Hua Qing Wen capsules were found to be effective in the treatment of COVID-19.
The main recovery rates in the treatment of VOVID-19 patients were observed in several provinces of China who used MCT in 87 per cent of their patients, including Gansu (63.7 per cent), Ningxia (50 per cent) and Hunan (50 per cent), while Hubei Province, which used MCT in only about 30 per cent of its patients with COVID-19, had the lowest recovery rate (13 per cent).
However, this is a fairly rough comparison, as many other impact factors, such as the amount and severity of the patients, should be included in the evaluation.
On February 18, 2020, Boli Zhang and his colleagues published a study comparing the treatment of Western medicine (U.S) only with the combined treatment of MO and MCT.
They discovered that the times necessary for the recovery of the body temperature, the disappearance of the symptoms and hospitalization were remarkably lower in the MOMCT group than in the MO group only.
More surprisingly, the worsening rate of symptoms (from mild to severe) was noticeably lower in the OMMCT group than in the MO group only (7,4 per cent versus 46.2 per cent) and mortality was lower in the MO-MCT group than in the MO group only (8.8 per cent versus 39 per cent).
However, the effectiveness and safety of MCT are still awaiting more, well-recognizable tests at larger scales and in more centres.
It would also be interesting to characterize the mechanism of the actions and to determine the effective components of MCT treatments or their combinations if possible.
Patients with suspicion or confirmation of COVID-19 are generally very scared of the highly contagious and even deadly disease, and people in quarantine also experience boring, loneliness and anger.
In addition, symptoms of the infection such as fever, hypoxia and cough, as well as the adverse effects of treatments, such as insomnia caused by corticosteroids, can cause more anxiety and mental anguish.
At the early stage of the SARS outbreak, a series of psychiatric morbidities were reported, including persistent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychomotor arousal, psychotic symptoms, delirium and even suicidal tendencies.
Mandatory contact tracing and quarantine, as part of public health responses to the COVID-19 outbreak, can cause people to feel more nervous and culprit because of the effects of contagion, quarantine and stigma on their families and friends.
Therefore, mental health care should be provided to patients with COVID-19, those with suspicion and people who are in contact with them, as well as the general public who need it.
Psychological support should include the formation of multidisciplinary mental health teams, clear communications with regular and accurate updates on the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak and treatment plans and the use of electronic devices and professional applications to avoid close contact between people.
Effective vaccines are essential to disrupt the transmission chain of infected animal and human revolving reservoirs to susceptible guests, and often complement anti-viral treatment in the control of epidemics caused by Emerging Visrus.
Efforts have been made to develop S-protein-based Vaccines to Generate Powerful, Long-duration Neutrative Antiquities or Proteinly Prombling Orleans or Immunity to protect against SARS-CoV.
Vaccines have been diagnosed with alive attenuated viruses on animal models for SARS.
However, the in-live efficacy of these possible vaccines in older people and lethal stimulation models and their protection against virus infection of Zonotic origin have yet to be determined before a clinical study begins.
That's probably because SARS became extinct 17 years ago and there have been no new cases since.
On the other hand, there are still sporadically MERS and conglomerates in the Middle East that spread to other regions due to the persistence of zoonotic sources in endemic areas.
Vaccination strategies have been developed for MERS using defused viruses, DNA plasmid, viral vectors, nanoparticles, virus-like particles and subunits of recombinant proteins, and some have been evaluated in animal models.
The development of a safe and effective SARS-CoV-2 vaccine for non-immune people is an urgent and decisive task to control the ongoing pandemic.
However, it is difficult to overcome difficulties due to the long period (18 months on average) necessary for the development of a vaccine and the dynamic variations of coronaviruses.
Because it is a new disease, COVID-19 has only just begun to manifest its entire clinical course in thousands of patients.
In most cases, patients can gradually retrieve without sequelae.
However, as with SARS and MERS, COVID-19 is also associated with high morbidity and mortality in patients with serious cases.
Creating a prognostic model for the disease is therefore essential for health agencies to prioritize their services, especially in areas with limited resources.
On the basis of clinical studies reported so far, the following factors may affect or be associated with the prognosis of patients with COVID-19 (Table (Table 33):
Age: The age was the most important factor for SARS prognosis, which is also successful for COVID-19.
COVID-19 occurred mostly in people between the ages of 30 and 45.7 per cent and 47.7 per cent of those patients were more than 50 years old in an 8866-per-year-previvor study, as described above.
Patients who were more likely to be in intensive care were comorbidities and constays and were considerably higher than those who did not require them (average age of 66 versus 51), suggesting that age is a pro prognostic factor for the evolution of patients with COVID-19.
Sex: SARS-CoV-2 has infected more men than women (0,31/100 000 versus 0.27/100 000), as described above.
Commodities and Complications: COVID-19 patients who require increased intensive care suffer from an acute cardiac lesion and arrhythmia.
Heart events were also the leading cause of death in SARS patients.
It has been reported that SARS-CoV-2 can also join positive ACE2 gluttages, which can lead to liver dysfunctions in patients with COVID-19.
It should be noted that age and underlying diseases are strongly correlated and can interfere with each other.
Absolute lab results: Reactive Pneumin (PCR) level in the blood reflects the severity of inflammation or tissue lesion and has been proposed as a possible prognostic factor for the disease, response to therapy and final recovery.
It has also been proposed the correlation between the level of RCP and the severity and the prognosis of COVID-19.
In addition, an elevated elctate level dehydrogenase (LDH), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and creatine kinase (CK) can also help predict the patient's evolution.
These enzymes are intensely expressed in several organs, especially the heart and liver, and are released when the tissue is damaged.
Therefore, they constitute traditional markers of cardiac or hepatic malfunctions.
Main clinical symptoms: Chest X-ray and the temporal progression of clinical symptoms should be considered in conjunction with other symptoms for the prediction of clinical outcomes and complications of COVID-19.
Steroid use: As described above, steroids are immunosuppressants that are commonly used as adjuvant therapy for infectious diseases in order to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
Since the high-dose use of corticosteroid was widespread in patients with Severe HRS, many survivors developed a vascular osteonecrosis with permanent incapacity and poor quality of life.
Therefore, steroid steroids should be used in low-dose doses for a brief period in patients with CIOVID-19.
Mental stress: As described above, during the COVID-19 outbreak, many patients have experienced extraordinary stress, as in general they endured long periods in quarantine and extreme uncertainty, and attended the deaths of close family members and other patients.
It is essential to provide psychotherapy and long-term support to these patients to help them recover from the stress and return to their normal lives.
According to the demographic studies carried out so far, COVID-19 seems to have epidemiological characteristics different from SARS.
In addition to being replicated in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 can be efficiently replicated in the upper respiratory tract and causes mild symptoms or does not cause symptoms in the early stage of the infection, as is the case with other coronaviruses that cause common colds.
As a result, infected patients at an early stage or incubation can produce a large number of viruses during their daily activities, which greatly hinders the control of the epidemic.
SARS-CoV transmission, however, was considered to occur when patients were seriously ill, while transmission mostly did not occur at the early stage.
Therefore, COVID-19's current outbreak is much more serious and difficult to control than the SARS outbreak.
Great efforts are currently being made in China, which includes the closure of Wuhan and surrounding cities and the continued quarantine of almost the entire population in the hope of disrupting SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
Although these measures have caused significant damage to the economy and other sectors of the country, the number of new patients is decreasing, indicating the slowdown of the epidemic.
According to the most optimistic forecast, the outbreak will be completed by March and the downward phase will last 3 to 4 months.
However, some other pundits aren't as optimistic.
Paul Hunter, et al., estimated that COVID-19, which appears to be considerably more infectious than SARS, won't be completed by 2020.
Ira Longini, et al., created a model to predict the evolution of the epidemic and suggested that SARS-CoV-2 could infect two-thirds of the world &apos; s population.
A Canadian group reported that SARS-CoV-2 was detected in both nasal and pharyngeal exudates in patients who had recovered and had left the hospital two weeks earlier, indicating that the newly identified virus could become a cyclic episode similar to influenza.
Promising signals, however, have been seen in China given the decrease in the number of new cases, indicating that current strategies could be working.
Emergically, Ebola was predicted to cause up to a million cases with half a million fatalities.
Strict quarantine and insolence, however, the disease has managed to be controlled.
The SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 may be weakened in terms of unhappiness and eventually extinction or become a less pathogenic virus that coexists with humans.
A comparison of the COVID-19 Epidemic with that of SARS and MERS is given below (fig. (fig. 55).
The SARS-CoV-2 is highly transmissible through cough or sneezing, and possibly also through direct contact with materials contaminated with the virus.
The stool virus was also detected, posing a new possibility of fecal-oral transmission.
A recent study of 138 cases reported that 41 per cent of the cases were possibly caused by nosoccosis infections, which includes 17 patients with other previous diseases and 40 health care providers.
Precautions should therefore be taken to protect humans, especially health-care providers, social workers, family workers, colleagues and even bystanders in contact with infected patients or people.
The first line of defense that could be used to decrease the risk of infection is the use of masks; the use of surgical masks and masks with respirator N95 (serial number 1860) helps to control the spread of the viruses.
Surgical mashes prevent fluid dripples of a potentially infected person from being transmitted through the air or adhering to the surfaces, from where they could be transmitted to other people.
However, only N95 masks (series number 1860) can protect against the inhalation of viriones as small as 10 to 80 nm, and only 5 per cent of viriones can penetrate them altogether; SARS-CoV-2 is similar to SARS-CoV in size and both measure around 85 nm.
Since particles can penetrate up to five stacked surgical masks, health care providers in direct contact with patients must wear N95 masks (series 1860) and not surgical masks.
In addition to masks, health care providers should wear beaus of bespoke insolation to further reduce contact with the viruses.
Vis viruses can also infect a person through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor became infected with SARS-CoV-2 despite using an N95 mask; the virus may have entered his or her body through his swollen eyes.
Therefore, health care providers should also we wear protective masks or transparent goggles while working with patients.
For the general public in areas affected or potentially affected, it is strongly suggested that the population wash their hands with disinfectant soaps more often than usual, try to voluntarily quarantine at home and limit contact with people who may be infected.
It's considered that three-foo-footing is a suitable distance for people to stay away from a patient.
These measures are effective methods to reduce the risk of infection and prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 emerged as a new virus for the human world, its high-profile SARS-CoV, reported on January 7, 2020, should have caused great alert in China for its experience with the SARS outbreak in 2003.
However, it was not until January 19, 2020 that the director of Wuhan's Center for Disease Control reassured citizens by saying that the new virus had low contagions and limited reproductive capacity among humans, and that there would be no problems to prevent and contain the disease.
"This message significantly relaxed the public's alarm, especially at a time when the whole country was preparing for the Spring Feast, and the critical moment to contain the disease on a minimal scale in Wuhan was missed.
China &apos; s disease control agencies should learn from this harsh lesson and make decisive improvements in the future.
For example, these agencies should be (1) more cautious in making public announcements, as every word matters to the citizens and can cause them to change their attitude or decisions; (2) more sensitive and reactive to unusual information from clinics, instead of waiting for formal reports from doctors or officials; (3) more restrictive to contain a possible epidemic at an early stage, rather than trying to reassure the public; and (4) more regular in the implementation of suitable and effective drills to increase public awareness of epidemic diseases and test and improve the response system of society periodically.
The COVID-19 outbreak caused by the new SARS-CoV-2 virus began at the end of December 2019.
In less than two months, it has spread throughout China and about 50 more countries from all over the world at the time of this analysis.
Since the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and there are also a lot of similarities between COVID-19 and SARS symptoms, the COVID-19 outbreak has generated the sensation of SARS reappearance.
However, there are some notorious differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are essential to contain the epidemic and treat patients.
COVID-19 affects older people more than young people and more to men than to women, and severity and mortality are also higher in older people than in young people.
SARS has a mortality rate greater than COVID-19 (10,91 % versus 1.44 per cent).
COVID-19 patients transmit the virus although they do not have any symptoms, while patients with SARS generally do so when they are seriously ill, which generates a much greater difficulty to contain the spread of COVID-19 compared to SARS.
This partly explains why the SARS-CoV-2 spread much faster and more broadly than SARS-CoV.
The regular RNA test for SARS-CoV-2 may be negative in some patients with CIOVID-19.
On the other hand, cured patients can test positive for the virus again.
These results drastically increase the risk that the virus will spread.
Given such rapid progress in COVID-19's investigation, a number of important issues remain to be resolved, such as:
Where did SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Although a 96 per cent of genetic homology was found between SARS-CoV-2 and two SARS-reminor-like bat wreaths, it is not yet possible to conclude that SARS-CoV-2 comes from the bats.
What animal was the intermediate species that transmitted the original host virus, let's say, the bats, the humans?
Without knowing the answers to number 1 and 2, it's not possible to brake the transmission efficiently, and the outbreak can resurface at any time.
Although molecular modelling and biochemistry tests have shown that SARS-CoV-2 binds to ACE2, how exactly does the virus penetrate airway cells and cause subsequent pathological changes?
Does the virus also bind to cells that express ACE2 in other organs?
Without clear answers to these questions, it is not possible to achieve a rapid and precise diagnosis and effective treatment.
How long is the epidemic going to last?
How does the virus genetically evolve while being transmitted among humans?
Will it turn into a global pandemic, extinct such as SARS or resupply periodically such as the flu?
Although it may take some time, it is essential to look for answers to these questions and to many others.
But whatever it costs, we don't have a choice but to stop the epidemic as soon as possible and go back to our normal life.
Zonotic virgins of human coronaviruses
The mutation and adaptation have boosted the co-evolution of the coronaviruses and their guests, including humans, for thousands of years.
Until 2003, it was known that two human coronaviruses caused mild diseases, such as the common cold.
Outbreaks of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle Eastern Renaissance Syndrome (MERS) have reversed the currency by revealing how devastating and deadly a corona-virus HIV infection can be.
SARS-CoV-2 in central China at the end of 2019 put coronaviruses back in the spotlight and we were struck with high transmissibility and reduced pathogenicity compared to his brother, SARS-CoV.
Human corona-virus infection is a Zonosis and it would be useful for us to know the zoonotic origins of human coronaviruses.
The majority of human coronaviruses have their origins in the bats, in which they are not pathogens.
There are also the guests of the intermediate reservoir of some human coronaviruses.
Identifying animal hostesses has direct implications for the prevention of diseases in humans.
Investigating the interactions between coronaviruses and guests in animals could also shed important information about the pathogeneity of coronaviruses in humans.
In this analysis, we present a general description of the existing knowledge of the seven coronaviruses of humans, with emphasis on the history of their discovery, as well as in their zoonotic origins and the transmission between species.
Remarkably, we compare and contrast the different coronavirus of humans from the perspective of the evolution of the virus and the recombination of the genome.
The current epidemic of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) is analyzed in this context.
In addition, they also highlight the requirements for successful guest changes and the implications for the evolution of the virus in the severity of the disease.
The coronaviruses belong to the Coronaviridae family, which includes a group of multicatenary RNA virus with wrapping.
These viruses, which house the largest genome, from 26 to 32 kilobases, among RNA viruses, have been dubbed "coronavirus" due to their morphology, due to the shape of a corona they have under an electron microscope.
In terms of their structure, coronaviruses have unsepulated genomes that share a similar organization.
Approximately two-thirds of the genome contains two large open reading frames (ORF1a and ORF1b), which are translated into pp1a and pp1ab replicatic polyproteins.
Polyproteins are further processed to generate 16 non-structural proprietary proteins called nsp1-16.
The remaining genome contains ORF for structural proprietary proteins, including spike (S), wrapping (E), membran (M) and nucleoprotein (N).
A number of specific accessory proteins for each lineage are also encoded by different coronavirus lineages.
Based on the difference in protein sequences, coronaviruses are classified into four genres (alphacoronavirus, betacoronavirus, gammacoronavirus and deltacoronaviruses), among which betacoronavirus genus contains most human coronaviruses and is subdivided into four lineages (A, B, C and D).
Cylogenetic evidence has shown that bats and rodents are the source of genes for most alphacoronaviruses and betacoronavirus, while birds are the main reservoir of gammacoronavirus and deltacoronavirus.
For Thousands of Years, Coronaviruses have constantly crossed species barriers and some have emerged as important human pathogens.
To this day, seven coronaviruses of humans have been known.
These include HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 are alphacoronavirus.
The other five betacoronaviruses are HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, coronavirus of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV), Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63 usually cause mild symptoms, such as a common cold or diarrhea.
On the other hand, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and recently identified SARS-CoV-2 are highly pathogenic and cause severe lower respiratory tract infection in a relatively larger number of patients, with a higher probability of developing acute respiratory distress syndrome (RASD) and extrapulmonary manifestations.
HCoV-229E, B814, was isolated from the nasal secretion of patients with a common cold in the mid-1960s.
Since then, more knowledge has accumulated through in-depth studies on HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, which cause self-limited symptoms.
In fact, up to the SARS outbreak, it was a widely accepted consecident that human coronavirus infection is generally harmless.
The SARS outburst in 2003 is one of the most devastating in today's history and infected more than 8000 people, with a gross lethality of around 10 per cent.
Ten years on, the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) outbreak resulted in a persistent sporadic sprinkeus-sprinking ecosystem peninsula to the rest of the world.
The new Magrovirus of Humans 2019 (2019-nCoV), later called SARS-CoV-2, is responsible for the ongoing coronavirus disease epidemic 2019 (COVID-19), which has claimed more than 3120 lives and infected more than 91,000 people as of March 3, 2020.
Alarm has been ringing and the world needs to prepare for the upcoming SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
The seven coronaviruses of humans have a Zonological origin in bats, mice or domestic animals.
Various lines of evidence support the evolutionary origin of all human coronaviruses in bats, in which the viruses are well adapted and non- pathogens, but have great genetic diversity.
The COVID-19 Epidemic has posed enormous medical, scientific, social and moral challenges in China and the rest of the world.
Tracing the Zonantic Origins of human coronaviruses provides a framework for understanding natural history, the driving force, and factors restricting the jump between species.
This could also guide or make it easier to securate the SARS-CoV-2 intermediate animal and amplifier guests, which would have important implications for preventing propagation in the future.
In this analyzing we present a general description of the zoonotic origins, the transmission between species and the pathogeneity of the coronaviruses of humans.
In particular, we highlight and look at the common issue that parental viruses of human coronaviruses are usually not pathogens in their natural reservoir guests, but become pathogens after transmitting between species to a new host.
We are also looking at the trend of the evolution of human coronaviruses, in which increased transmissibility usually leads to a decrease in pathogenicity.
The evolution of the ongoing SARS-CoV-2 outbreak is also analyzed in this context.
It's been known about animal coronaviruses since the late 1930s.
Before the B814 strata of HCoV-229E was isolated for the first time from the nose secretion of patients who had contracted a common slur, different coronaviruses had been isolated in different infected animals such as turkey, mouse, cow, pork, cat and dog.
In the last few decades, seven coronaviruses of humans have been identified.
A brief summary of the history of the discoveries of human coronaviruses in chronological order (Table 1) would be informative and instructive.
The first HCoV-229E strain was insulated from the respiratory tract of patients with upper respiratory tract infection in 1966 and was subsequently adapted to grow on WI-38 lung cell lines.
Patients infected with HCoV-229E had common cold symptoms, such as headache, sneezing, general discomfort and throat sorrow, fever and cough in 10-20 per cent of cases.
Later, in 1967, HCoV-OC43 isolated itself from organ culture and subsequent serial passage in the brains of lactating mice.
The clinical characteristics of HCoV-OC43 infection appear to be similar to those caused by HCoV-229E, the symptoms of which are identical to infections by other respiratory pathogens such as influenza A viruses and rhinoviruses.
Both the HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are distributed around the world and tend to be transmitted predominantly during the Winter season, in temperate climates.
In general, the incubation time of these two viruses is less than a week, followed by a period of illness of about 2 weeks.
According to a study with human volunteers, healthy people infected with HCoV-229E developed a mild common cold.
Only a few immunocompromised patients had a serious lower respiratory junction.
SARS, also known as an atypical pneumonia, caused the first pandemic caused by a well-documented human coronavirus in the history of humanity, and the etiological agent is SARS-CoV, the third human coronavirus that was discovered.
SARS's first case dates back at the end of 2002 in Guangdong Province, China.
The SARS Epidemic resulted in 8096 reported cases with 774 deaths and spread across many countries and continents.
Apart from superpropagators, it is estimated that each case could cause approximately two secondary cases, with an incubation period of 4 to 7 days and the appearance of the maximum viral load to the tenth day of the disease.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially have mialgia, headache, fever, general discomfort and chills, followed by dyspnea, coughing and breathing difficulties such as late symptoms.
Linfopenia, altered liver function and high level of creatine kinase are common abnormal results in SARS lab tests.
diffuse alveolar damage, proliferation of epithelial cells and an increase in macrophages are also observed in SARS patients.
Approximately 20-30 per cent of patients subsequently require intensive care and mechanical respiratory assistance.
In addition to the lower respiratory tract, a number of organs, including the gastrointestinal tract, the liver and the kidney, can also become infected in these serious cases, usually accompanied by a cycytokine storm, which could be fatal, particularly in immunosuppressed patients.
The virus first isolated itself from the open-pit lung biopsy of a family member of the initial patient who traveled to Hong Kong from Guangzhou.
Since then, enormous efforts have been devoted to the investigation of the coronaviruses of humans.
The HCoV-NL63 was isolated from a seven-month-old from the Netherlands at the end of 2004.
Initially, it was determined to be prevalent in young children, older adults and immunocompromised patients with respiratory diseases.
Cholk, conjunctivitis, fever and bronchiolitis is common in the disease caused by HCoV-NL63.
Another independent study described the isolation of the same virus from a nasal sample taken from an eight-month-old boy suffering from pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although it was identified in the Netherlands, it's actually distributed around the world.
HCoV-NL63 has been estimated to cause approximately 4.7 per cent of common respiratory diseases and their maximum incidence occurs in early summer, spring and winter.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, also known as crup.
In the same year, the HCoV-HKU1 isolated from a 71-year-old man who had been hospitalized with pneumonia and bronchiolitis in Hong Kong.
In addition to community-acquired pneumonia and bronchiolitis, HCoV-HKU1 was reported to be associated with acute asthma exacerbations.
Like HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 was around the world and caused mild respiratory diseases.
These four coronaviruses of humans acquired in the community have adapted well to humans and, in general, are less likely to mutate to cause highly pathogenic diseases, although there have been accidents due to unknown causes, such as in the uncommon case of a more virulent HCoV-NL63 subtype, which was recently reported to have caused serious lower respiratory tract infection in China.
In general, when these coronaviruses of humans acquire the ability to be transmitted efficiently efficiently and continuously maintained in humans, they also become less virulent or pathogenic.
MERS-CoV was first isolated in 2012 from the lung of a 60-year-year-old patient who had developed acute pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia.
Although the majority of laboratory-confirmed cases are originating in the Middle East, there have been cases imported with occasional contagion to close contacts in a number of European countries and Tunisia.
Another secondary outbreak occurred in South Korea in 2015 with 186 confirmed cases.
MERS Clinical Demonstrations Resemble SARS Destinations, characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
Unlike SARS, many patients with MERS have also developed acute kidney failure, which so far occurs only with MERS among diseases caused by coronaviruses in humans.
More than 30 per cent of patients have gastrointestinal sympathisurities such as diarrhea and vomit.
As at February 14, 2020, there were more than 2,500 laboratory-conffirmed cases, with a high fatality of 34.4 per cent, making MERS-CoV one of the most devastating viruses for humans known.
Conglomerates of Pneumonia patients were detected in Wuhan, Hubei Province of China from midway to the end of December 2019, which retrospectively joined SARS-CoV-2.
The World Health Organization declared an ongoing outdoor respiratory tract infection caused by SARS-CoV-2 a public health emergency of international importance and also called COVID-19 the disease.
As of March 3, 2020, 90 053 cases have been confirmed around the world, with a rough fatality of 3.4 per cent.
Remarkably, letality in Hubei, China, it is 4.2 per cent, while outside it is 1.2 per cent.
SARS-CoV-2 causes severe respiratory infection, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which occurs as a fever, cough and dyspnea.
Diarrhea has also been observed in some patients.
Pneumonia is one of the most serious symptoms and can rapidly evolve to acute respiratory distress syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very alike due to an 82 % high nucleotide sequential homology, they are categorized in different branches of the phylogenetic tree.
SARS-CoV-2 is apparently less pathogenic but more transmissible compared to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
There have been reports of asymptomatic people infected with SARS-CoV-2, which could contribute to its rapid spread around the world.
Comparing SARS-CoV-2 with the other six human coronaviruses, similarities and differences of great interest are identified.
First of all, the incubation period and the duration of human coronavirus disease are very similar.
The SARS-CoV-2 in this regard follows the general trend of the other six human coronaviruses.
Secondly, the severity of COVID-19 Symptoms is between SARS-CoV and the four wreath-stricken humans acquired in the community (i.e. HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL6).
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 infection has characteristics that are most frequently observed in coronavirus infections of humans acquired in the community, which includes the presentation of non-specific or mild symptoms or even the absence of symptoms.
On the other hand, there is also a small subgroup of severe cases of COVID-19 as is the case with SARS-CoV infection, although the relationship is a little lower.
Thirdly, SARS-CoV-2 Transmission also shows interesting patterns characteristic of both human coronaviruses acquired in the community and SARS-CoV.
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 transmissibility is at least as tall as the coronaviruses of humans acquired in the community.
On the other hand, it remains to be verified if SARS-CoV-2 transmissibility decreases after passes between humans, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Ultimately, as with the other human coronavirus, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in faecal samples.
If SARS-CoV-2 fecal-oral transmission plays an important role, such as SARS-CoV, at least in a few circumstances, it still needs to be clarified in future studies.
It is also of particular interest to find out whether SARS-CoV-2 could exhibit seasonality, as is the case with human coronaviruses acquired in the community.
However, SARS-CoV-2 characteristics, including its transmissibility, pathogenicity and sustainable propagation after human passes, will have an impact on the final outcome of the ongoing COVID-19 outbreak.
The four coronaviruses of humans acquired in the community that cause mild symptoms have been well adapted to humans.
From a different perspective, it could also be true that humans have adapted well to these four coronaviruses of humans.
In other words, both could be survivors of ancient human coronavirus pandemics.
Coronaviruses have been removed from humans that cause serious diseases in humans and humans who developed serious corona-virus diseases in humans.
For this to happen, human coronaviruses must be replicated in humans to a sufficient extent to allow the accumulation of adaptive mutations that contrast host restriction factors.
In this sense, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak persists and the more people it infects, the greater the likelihood that it will adapt completely to humans.
If adapted well, its transmission in humans would be difficult to stop through quarantine or other infection control measures.
For many years, the four coronaviruses acquired in the community have circulated in human populations and have caused common colds in immunocompetent people.
These viruses don't need an animal reservoir.
In contrast, SARS-CoV and highly pathogenic MERS-CoV have not adapted well for humans and their transmission between humans is unsustainable.
They need to stay and propagate in their zoonotic reservoirs and look for the opportunity to spread to susceptible human goals, possibly through one or more intermediate guests and amplifiers.
The SARS-CoV-2 has similar characteristics to both SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV as well as to the four community-accompanguing human coronavirus.
It is highly transmissible such as the coronaviruses of humans acquired in the community, at least at the moment.
However, it's more pathogenic than human coronaviruses acquired in the community and less pathogenic than SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV.
It remains to be seen whether it will fully adapt to humans and will circulate among humans without an animal reservoir or intermediate guest.
Before analyzing the animal origins of human coronavirus, it will be useful for us to examine the definitions and characteristics of the evolutionary, natural, resold, interns and amplifier amplifiers of human coronaviruses.
An animal acts as an evolutionary host of a human coronavirus if it houses a closely related ancestor who shares a tall homology at the nucleotide sequence level.
The ancestral virus is usually well adapted and isn't pathogenic in this host.
In a similar way, a reservoir guest houses a wreath-lipping of humans continuously and in the long term.
In both cases, guests become naturally infected and are the natural hostesses of human coronavirus or their parental virus.
On the other hand, if the coronavirus of humans was introduced to the intermediate host just before or at almost the same time as its introduction into humans, it is not well adapted to the new host and is usually pathogenic.
Intermediate guests can act as the zoonotic source of the infection in humans and fulfil the function of amplifier host by allowing the virus to replicate temporarily and then transmitted to humans to amplify the scale of the infection in humans.
A coronavirus of humans can cause an infection in the terminal host if you can't sustain its transmission in the intermediate host.
On the contrary, human coronaviruses can also be adapted to the mid-to-mid-to-assimorants and even generate long-term endemicity.
In this case, the intermediate guest becomes a natural reservoir host.
Epidemiological data retrospectively revealed that SARS's initial case had had contact with hunting animals.
Subsequent research on seroprevalence indicated that animal traders had a higher prevalence of anti-SARS-CoV IgG compared to that of the general population.
Civetas of masked palm trees (Paguma larvata) and a raccoon dog in live animal markets were the first identified carriers of SARS-CoV-like viruses that are almost identical to SARS-CoV.
This was indirectly supported by the fact that there were no other SARS cases recorded after killing every crank in the markets.
It was reported, however, that civetas from wild masked palms or farms without exposure to live animal markets were mostly negative in SARS-CoV, which suggests that masquerading palm clives would only act as an amplifiant intermediate guest, but not as a natural SARS-CoV reservoir.
Remarkably, since 80 per cent of the various animals in Guangzhou markets have anti-SARS-CoV antibodies cannot be ruled out the chances that several small mammammal species may also act as SARS-CoV intermediate amplifier guests.
All of them appear to be terminal guests of SARS-CoV.
Later searches of SARS-CoV's natural animal guest revealed a closely related bat wreath, called SARS Rhinolophus Rhinolophus-connected Rhinolophus (SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3), which exists in Chinese horseshoe bats.
These bats are positive in anti-SARS-CoV antipoonts and SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3 genome sequencing.
This and other bat wreaths share an 88-92 % nucleotide sequential homology with SARS-CoV.
These studies have lay the foundation for the new concept that bats house emerging pathogens in humans.
Several SARS (SL-CoV)-like coronaviruses have also been identified in bats, but none, except one called WIV1, can insolate themselves as a living virus.
It's known that the angiotensin 2 converting enzyme (ACE2) is the SARS-CoV receptor.
WIV1 obtained from a faecal sample of bat was shown to use ACE2 of bats, cranks and humans as a receiver to enter the cells.
Astonishingly, serum serums from convalescent SARS patients were able to neutralise WIV1.
So far, the WIV1 represents SARS-CoV's most closely related ancestor on bats, which shares a 95 per cent nucleotide sequential homology.
In spite of the high homologation between these two viruses, WIV1 is generally believed not to be SARS-CoV's immediate parental virus and that the bats are not SARS-CoV's Immediate Reserium Host.
According to phylogenetic analytic analysis, MERS-CoV is categorized in the same group as bat CoV-HKU4.
The Bat CoV-HKU4 and MERS-CoV use the same host receiver, dipeptidil peptidase 4 (DPP4), to enter the virus.
MERS-CoV RNA-dependent RNA sequees phylogetically come closer to their bat betacoronavirus counterparts identified in Europe and Africa.
So far, you cann't find an alive MERS-CoV on savages.
MERS-CoV and its closest kinswoman, Bat CoV-HKU25, share a nucleotide sequential homology of just 87 %.
Therefore, the bat might not be MERS-CoV's Immediate Reserium host.
On the other hand, studies in the Middle East have shown that dromedaries are HIV-nephant in MERS-CoV neutralizing antibodies specifically, as well as camels originating in the Middle East in several African countries.
An MERS-CoV alive identical to the virus found in humans was isolated from dromedary nasal exudate, supporting even more that camels act as bona fide reschedulers from MERS-CoV.
It should also be noted that, in general, mild symptom symptoms were observed, but a massive propagation of the virus in infected camels for MERS-CoV research.
Remarkably, the infected camels propagate the viruses not only by airway, but also by fecal-oral route, which is also the main route of virus propagation from bats.
However, there are still any questions, as many confirmed cases of MERS do not have a history of contact with camels prior to the appearance of symptoms, which is reasonably attributed to transmission between humans or unknown transmission pathways involving unrecognizable animal species hosting MERS-CoV.
The SARS-CoV-2 shares a 96.2 % nucleotide homology with a bat wreath, RaTG13, insulated from Rhinolophus affinis bat.
As with SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV cases, sequential diverence between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is too large to assign a parental relationship.
In other words, the bats may not be SARS-CoV-2's immediate reserve guests unless they are found in the near-identical wretch in the up-and-governed bat coronavirus.
Presumably SARS-CoV-2 mid-eating animal guests should be among the wild species that are being sold and killed in Huanan's seafood wholesale market, with which many of COVID-19's initial cases were associated, indicating a likely animal-to-to-human event.
Several recent studies based on metagenomic sequencing have suggested that a group of small endangered mammal mammals, known as pangolines (Manis javanica), could also harbor SARS-CoV-2 ancestral betacoronaviruses.
The genomes of this new pangolines coronavirus share a nucleotide sequential homology of 85-92 % with SARS-CoV-2.
However, they are just as closely related to the RaTG13, with an identity of about 90 per cent at nucleotide sequential level.
They are grouped into two SARS-CoV-2-like sub-lineages on the phylogenetic tree, one of which shares a receptor-binding domain (RBD) most similar to SARS-CoV-2, with an amino acid sequential identity of 97.4 per cent.
In stark contrast, SARS-CoV-2 RBD and RaTG13 are more diverging, despite a higher degree of sequential homology throughout the genome.
A previous study in sick pangolines also reported the detection of viral contigos in lung samples, which turn out to be equally related to SARS-CoV-2.
This study adopted different methods of assemblaging and manual healing to generate a partial genome sequencing that comprised about 86.3 per cent of the viral genome in full length.
We can't rule out the possibility that the pangolin will be one of SARS-CoV-2's mid-animal guests.
At present, however, there is no evidence to support a direct origin in SARS-CoV-2 pangolin due to sequential divergence between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-2 pangoline-related betacoronavirus.
In addition, the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is even lower than between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-2-related pangoon betacoronavirus.
SARS-CoV-2's evolutionary route in bats, pangolines and other mammals has yet to be established.
Although the highest sequential homology was found in RBDs between SARS-CoV-2 and pangolin, betacoronaviruses associated with SARS-CoV-2, SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 share the highest complete genome sequence homology.
It is highly speculative that the high degree of similarity between RBDs of pangolin-related betacoronaviruses associated with SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-2 is due to converging evolution mediated by selectivity.
A counter-proposal defends a rebubration between SARS-CoV-2-related pangoon betacoronavirus and the RaTG13 in the third species of wild animal.
As an impulsive force in evolution, the recombination is widespread among betacoronaviruses.
There has not yet been a conclusion on SARS-CoV-2's immediate zoognostic origin.
In addition to highly pathogenic human coronaviruses, it also studied the zoonotic origin of HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-HKU1.
Cylogenetic evidence indicated that both HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-229E may have originated from bat coronaviruses, while HCoV-OC43 and HCoV-H-HKU1 parental viruses have been found in rodents.
It was reported that a bat wreath called ARCoV.2 (coronavirus of the Appalachian mounts) detected in a tricolor bat of North America presented a close relationship with HCoV-NL63.
On the other hand, the HCoV-229E was genetically related to another bat wretch, called Hipposideros/GhanaKwam/19/2008, which was detected in Ghana, although strenides have also been suspected as intermediate guests.
For greater clarity, the knowledge available so far of the animal origins of known human coronaviruses is summarized in Figure 1 and Table 2.
The phylogenetic analytic analysis has provided evidence of coronavirus transmission of humans between species in history.
When HCoV-OC43 crossed between species to infecting humans from livestock animals in 1890, there was a pandemic of respiratory infection.
HCoV-229E's species-to-wheel-drive history is not so accurately known.
Alphacoronavirus of bats have been found closely linked to the HCoV-229E.
Among them there's an alpacas alphacoronavirus.
Several lines of evidence support the transmission of the virus from bat to humans directly.
In the first place, unlike alpacas, humans could have contact with the bats in a shared ecological niche.
On the other hand, humans have close contact with alpacas.
On the other hand, bat alphacoronaviruses associated with HCoV-229E are diverse and non-pathogenic in the bats, while alpacas alphacoronaviruses caused an outbreak of respiratory disease in the infected animals.
Ultimate Alpacas alfacoronavirus has not finally been found in wild animals.
Therefore, it is not possible to rule out the possibility of alpacas acquiring the alphacoronavirus related to the HCoV-229E of humans.
In fact, the bats are the direct source of pathogenic viruses in humans, such as rabies virus, Ebola Virus, Nipah Virus and Hendra Virus.
Therefore, it wouldn't be too surprising for the bats to transmit the HCoV-229E directly to humans.
Alternatively, while bat alphacoronaviruses act as a genetic collection of HCoV-229E, alpacas and dromedaries could act as intermediate guests who transmit the viruses to humans, in particular in the case of MERS-CoV.
The MERS-CoV serves as an excellent example of inter-species-to-wheel-to-wheel-dropping bats, dromedaries.
MERS-CoV's evolutionary origin in the bats is undiscovered with its initial identification, and has also been supported by subsequent findings.
It is clear that the bats contribute, with a profuse collection of virus species, to the exchange of genetic fragments between species and the transmission between species.
Longevity, densely populous colonies, close social interplay and great ability to float are all favorable conditions that make bats the ideal "virus sprinkler."
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has been introduced to dromedaries for decades.
It is well adapted to these camels, who have gone from being an intermediate guest, to being a stable and natural reservoir guest.
MERS-CoV causes very mild illness and maintains a relatively low mutation rate in these animals.
Its sporadic transmission to humans is an accident and humans continue to be a terminal guest of MERS-CoV, as the transmission is unsustainable.
Compared to the camel's role in the MERS-CoV transmission, the pangolines &apos; role, if any, in SARS-CoV-2 transmission is different.
Pangolin betacoronavirus is particularly highly pathogenic in pangolines.
They may be a terminal guest of SARS-CoV-2-related betacoronaviruses, such as civetas in the SARS-CoV case.
A number of SARS-CoV-2 species transmission from animals to human should be confirmed or discarded in future studies.
In the first place, the batsmen could be the reservoir host of an SARS-CoV-2-related virus, almost identical to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans would be able to share the ecological niche with the bats, through carnage or coal mining.
Secondly, pangolines could be one of the intermediate amplifier guests in which an SARS-CoV-2-related virus had recently been introduced.
Humans contract the virus through the killing and consumption of game meat.
Many mammal mammals, including domestic animals, may be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2.
An anti-antibody study is needed in domestic and wild animals.
Thirdly, as mentioned above, the recombination and adaptation of the SARS-CoV-2 could have occurred in a third species that has contact with both the bats and the pangolines.
Searching for SARS-CoV-2 animal origins continues.
Apart from the different types of animal guests, three main factors in viral conditions also play an important role in making it easier for coronaviruses to cross species barriers.
In the first place, its relatively high mutation rates in RNA replication.
Compared to other monocatenary RNA viruses, the estimated mutation rates of the coronaviruses could be considered "Moderate to high," with an average replacement rate of 10-4 substitutions per year, by site 2, according to the coronavirus adaptation phase to new guests.
The coronaviruses have an exorribuclease of reading verification, whose elimination results in an extremely high mutability and attenuation or even unfeasibility.
Interestingly, it is known that the nucleotide analyrium remdesivir suppresses coronavirus replication by inhibiting this exorribonuclease and RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.
Remdesivir is one of the most promising anti-SARS-CoV-2 agents to have been tested in clinical trials.
However, the mutation rates of the coronaviruses are about a million times higher than those of their guests.
In addition, the mutati mutation rate is usually high when coronaviruses aren't well-appropriated to the host.
Compared to SARS-CoV, which has a high mutation rate, SARS-CoV-2 mutation rate is apparently lower, suggesting a higher level of human adaptation.
It's hoped it's already been adapted to another host close to humans.
In addition to the SARS-CoV-2, this also applies to MERS-CoV, which is well adapted to dromedaries.
In theory, the genetic drift is unlikely to cause vaccines and antivirals against SARS-CoV-2 to lose their effectiveness quickly.
On the other hand, the long RNA genome of coronaviruses exerts additional plasticity in the modification of the genome for mutations and recombination and thus increases the probability of co-evolution between species, which facilitates the emergence of new coronaviruses when the right conditions occur.
This is supported by the unique copious open reading frames and the functions of the proteins encoded in the 3' direction of the genome.
In the third place, the coronaviruses change randomly and often from templates during RNA replication using a unique mechanism of "copy-choice."
In a guest that acts as a mixing vehicle, the string change occurs frequently during coronavirus RNA transcribing.
RNA of a full length and subgenomics could be rebuilt to generate new coronaviruses.
Physiogenetic evidence of natural recombination has been found in HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-OC43, as well as in coronaviruses of animals such as Bat SL-Co-CoV and Bat CoV-HKU9.
Visor-guest interaction in relation to transmission
In addition to the three viral factors mentioned above, the viral interaction with the receiver is another key factor that has an influence on the transmission between species.
At present, the SARS-CoV recombination is taken as a typical example, which also showed evidence of positive selection in inter-species transmission events.
Based on the comparative analysis between isolated SARS-CoV strains of humans and cavtas, SARS-CoV is believed to experience rapid adaptation in different hosts, in particular with mutations in protein S RBD.
In general, RBD of a coronavirus S protein interacts with the cellular receiver and is intensely selected by the host's antiquity response.
In SARS-CoV, the RBD is located in amino acids 318 to 510 in fragment S1, which binds to human ACE2 and its curceptors for viral input.
SARS-CoV RBD is able to recognize the ACE2 receptors of several animals, including bat, cave, mouse and raccoon dog, which allows transmission between species of the virus.
In fact, it was observed that only 6 amino acid residues were different from the isolated strains of human viruses and civets in the RBD, and 4 of them are in the receptor-binding motif for interaction with the ACE receptor2.
Civeta SARS-CoV has K479N and S487T mutations in its RBD, which could increase the affinity of spike protein interaction with the ACE receptor2.
In other words, these two amino acid substitutions could be central to adapting the virus to humans.
It should be noted that SARS-CoV-2 shares the same cellular receiver with SARS-CoV.
A 30% difference between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV in unit S1 of Protein S implies that affinity for linking protein S with human ACE2 could have been altered.
In fact, an electronic cryomicroscopy study points to the affinity 10 to 20 times greater than this binding compared to that of human ACE2 and SARS-CoV Protein S.
It will also be of interest to determine whether any other co-receptors might be necessary for SARS-CoV-2 transmission.
Unsurprisingly, the HCoV-NL63 also joins ACE2, but with a different part of S.
There's plenty of other coronavirus receivers from humans, such as aminopeptidase N in HCoV-229E and 9-O-O-acetylated silicon acid in HCoV-OC43.
They may also be responsible for the successful adaptation of these coronaviruses in humans, after the transmission between species from their animal guests.
In addition to cellular receiverees, the result of human corona-vehicle-to-genuic transmission is also determined by other factors of dependence and host restriction.
Divergence of these host proteins between humans and natural reservoir hosts of human coronaviruses, such as bats, dromedaries and rodents, could constitute an obstacle to inter-species transmission.
Coronaviruses of humans must usurp guest dependence factors and subvert host restriction factors to make transmission between species successful.
In this regard, the molecular determinants in this important area of virus-guest interaction still need to be identified and characterized.
An objective complete genome investigation of the dependency and refence factors for SARS-CoV-2 could be beneficial using advanced CRISPR technology.
Appearance of new human coronaviruses: let's leave from scratch
The diversity of bat coronaviruses provides numerous opportunities for the emergence of new coronavirus from humans.
In this sense, bat coronaviruses act as the genetic affinetic collection of human coronaviruses.
In addition, the rapid mutation and genetic recombination, they also promote the evolution of human coronaviruses and constitute two important steps in this process.
For example, the acquisition or loss of new proprietary encoding genes have the potential to drastically modify viral phenotypes.
Among SARS-CoV Accessory Proteins, ORF8 has been considered to be important in adapting to humans, as bat viruses related to SARS-CoV were isolated, but it was determined to encode divergent ORF8 proteins.
An elimination of 29 nucleotides characteristic of SARS-CoV was found in isolated strains at the beginning of the epidemic in humans.
This elimination divides the ORF8 in ORF8a and ORF8b, and is believed to be an adaptive mutation that promotes guest change.
In addition, SARS-CoV has a possible recombination history with alpha and gammacoronavirus lineages, in which a large number of smaller recombinant regions were identified in RNA-dependent polymerase.
Recombration locations were also identified in the nsp9, the majority of the nsp10 and parts of the nsp14.
In the same way, it has been demonstrated that epidemic MERS-CoV experienced recombination events between different lineages, which occurred in dromedaries in Saudi Arabia.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, recombination events have also been observed in other human coronaviruses, in which human coronaviruses are recombined with other animal coronaviruses in their non-structural genes.
It should also be noted that artificial selection can contribute to unwanted changes in viral genomes, which most likely will result from freeing viruses from the selection pressures exerted, for example, by the guest's immune system.
An example of these effects is the loss of an ORF4 of a full-length ORF4 in the prototype HCoV-229E strain, due to an elimination of two nucleotides.
Although unto-yvertiated ORF4 could have been observed in bat and camel-licto viruses related to HCoV-229E, Alpacas alphacoronavirus presents a single nucleotide insertion, which causes a change of frame.
Ultimately, though no less importantly, the evolution of the new human coronaviruses is also driven by the selection pressure in their reservoir guests.
Symptoms were not detected or only mild symptoms were detected when coronavirus bats were infected, indicating mutual adaptation between coronaviruses and bats.
Apparently, the bats are well-appropriated to anatomical and physiological coronaviruses.
For example, defects in the activation of the pro-flammatory response in the bats efficiently reduce the pathology caused by coronaviruses.
In addition, the activity of natural killer cells in the bats is suppressed due to the positive regulation of the inhibitory receptor of natural killer cells NKG2/CD94 and the low level of expression of the Class I molecules of the major histocompatibility complex.
In addition, the high level of reactive oxygen (ROS) species generated from the high metabolic activity of the bats could suppress the replication of the coronavirus and affect the reading test of exorribonuclease, which provides the selection pressure for the generation of highly pathogenic strains of viruses when introduced into a new host.
More pathogenic coronavirus sacks could also evolve by recombination, which would lead to the acquisition of new proteins or protein characteristics for adaptation to the host.
It's therefore no coincidence that three new human coronaviruses have appeared in the last two decades.
Coronaviruses are not pathogenic or cause mild symptoms in their reservoir guests, such as bats and camels.
They vigorously replicate themselves without provoking a lofty immune response from the host.
In this lie the secrets of why there are asymptomatic carriers and what causes serious cases of infection in humans.
Severe symptoms are mainly due to the overactivation of the Immune response and cytokine storm, in which the stronger the immune response, the more serious the lung damage.
On the other hand, in asymptomatic wearers, the immune response disassociated itself from coronavirus replication.
The same strategy of disassociation of the immune response could have beneficial effects on anti-SARS-CoV-2 therapy.
Interfereon's response is particularly strong in the bats.
Therefore, the administration of type I interferon, at least at the early stage of SARS-CoV-2 infection in humans, should be beneficial.
In addition, the activation of NLRP3 hinomama in the bats is defective.
According to this reasoning, the inhibition of NLRP3 hinade with MCC950 could be useful in the treatment of COVID-19.
SARS-CoV-2's appearance follows the overall line that led to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV appearance.
Although a beat beta-coronavirus has been found that shares a 95 percent nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV, there's also a bat wretch that shares a 96 percent nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV-2.
Although civetas and other market animals have been found to host viruses identical to SARS-CoV, there have been no immediate SARS-CoV-2 intermediate guests identified.
Preangolin betacoronaviruses have been found to the SARS-CoV-2, indicating that pangolines could act as one of the intermediate hosts or that pangolines betacoronaviruses could bring gene fragments to the final SARS-CoV-2 version.
Although questions remain, there's no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 is man-made, either deliberately or by accident.
Coronaviruses have regained prominence due to the recent SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.
The study of coronaviruses in bats and other animals has profoundly changed our perception of the importance of the zoonotic origins and animal reservoirs of human coronaviruses in human transmission.
Vasta has shown that SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 originated in the bats and are transmitted to humans through mid-advisers.
Since SARS-CoV infection originates from contact between humans and clives in the markets, closing wet markets and killing cranks in them could have been effective in curbing the SARS epidemic.
Following the same reason, pangolines should be withdrawn from wet markets to prevent Zonantic transmission, in view of the discovery of several lineages of pangolines closely linked to SARS-CoV-2 related to SARS-CoV-2.
However, whether and how SARS-CoV-2 is transmitted to humans through pangolines and other mammals remains to elucidate in future research.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has existed in dromedaries for a long time.
These camels are an important transportation tool, as well as a main source of meat, milk, leather and wool for the local population.
They are widely spread across the Middle East and Africa.
It is therefore impossible to sacrifice all camels to control MERS, as was done in China with wild animal markets to prevent the spread of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2.
To prevent recurrent outbreaks of MERS, a comprehensive approach should be taken to develop effective MERS-CoV vaccines for camels, in combination with other infection control measures.
Since we're not being able to eliminate these viruses, new genotypes that cause sprouts may appear.
A variety of zoonotic coronaviruses are circulating in the wild.
In particular, bat coronaviruses with zoonotic potential are very diverse.
There's a great possibility that these zoonotic coronaviruses will evolve and rebuilt, which would result in the emergence of new coronaviruses that become more communicable or deadlier in humans in the future.
The culture of eating wild animals in some parts of China should be abandoned to reduce unnecessary contact between humans and animals.
Based on bad experiences with SARS, MERS and COVID-19, a better preparedness and response plan should be adopted.
In fact, many viruses have existed on the planet for a very long time.
They remain in their own natural reservoirs until there is an opportunity to propagate.
Although the bats have many characteristics that favor the spread of viruses, opportunities for human contact with bats and other wild species can be minimized by educating the population to move away from them.
Continuous surveillance of mammals is necessary to better understand the ecology of Coronaviruses and their natural guests, which will be useful to prevent the transmission from animal to human and future shoots.
In conclusion, the most effective way to prevent viral zoonoses is for humans to stay away from ecological niches in the natural reservoirs of zoonotic viruses.
There are still several pieces left in the pussy puzzle of SARS-CoV-2.
In the first place, if the bats transmit an ancestral SARS-CoV-2 virus to pangolines, it will be interesting analyzing under what circumstances the bats and pangolines might share the same ecological niche.
Secondly, if the bats have a more direct role in human transmission, it must be determined how humans come into contact with the bats.
Thirdly, if a third mammal acts as the true intermediate host, it must be echoed how it interacts with the different species, including humans, bats and pangolines.
Finally, since many mammals, including domestic animals, could be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, surveillance and experimental infection must be carried out.
Either it's a bat, a pangolin or another mammal, the SARS-CoV-2 or its parental viruses that are nearly identical are expected to be identified in its natural guests in the future.
Continuous investigations in this area will elucidate SARS-CoV-2's evolutionary route in animals, with important implications for the prevention and control of COVID-19 in humans.
It is necessary to update the diagnostic criteria of the suspected cases and confirmed cases of COVID-19
On February 6, 2020, our team published a quick advice guideline for the diagnosis and treatment of infection by the new coronavirus 2019 (2019-nCoV), and this guideline included our experience and constitutes a good reference for combating this pandemic around the world.
However, coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) is a new disease, our awareness and our knowledge gradually increases based on the results of ongoing research and experience in clinical practice; therefore, diagnostic and treatment strategies are also continuously updated.
In this letter, we responded to a comment on our guideline and provide the most recent diagnostic criteria of "suspect case," and "concerned," according to the latest Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of COVID-19 (seventh edition) published by the National Health Committee of the People &apos; s Republic of the Chinese People &apos; s Republic.
In December 2019, the new coronavirus 2019 (2019-nCoV) caused an outbreak, which is now officially known as coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and the virus was called coronavirus 2 of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, 2020, WHO described COVID-19 as a pandemic.
To combat SARS-CoV-2 infection, our team developed a quick counselling guideline that was published online at Military Medical Research on February 6, 2020.
He's attracted plenty of attention since it's been published.
It should be noted, however, that COVID-19 is a new disease, our awareness and our knowledge is gradually increasing based on the results of ongoing research and experience in clinical practice; therefore, diagnostic and treatment strategies are also continuously updated.
For example, the Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of COVID-19s published by the National Health Committee of the People's Republic of China (http://www.nhc.gov.cn/), between January 16 January 2020 and March 3, 2020, have published a total of seven editions and some contexts changed considerably.
Now, our directive received a comment from Zhou et al., they presented a simple classification proposal based on their clinical experience.
His work added new evidence for our directive and is also a valuable reference to this pandemic around the world.
We support his important work and express our appreciation.
However, his work also needs to be upgraded according to the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment (seventh version of the essay) and recent studies.
According to the seventh edition (March 23, 2020), it is necessary to confirm a suspicious case it is necessary to combine any point of epidemiological background characteristics with two points of clinical manifestations to do an in-depth analysis, or there must be three points of clinical manifestations if there is no clear epidemiological history:
Epidemiological history: (1) history of travel or residence in the city of Wuhan and the surrounding areas, or other communities in which cases of COVID-19 have been reported in the last 14 days prior to the appearance of the symptoms; (2) a history of contact with Infectious SARS-CoV-2 cases (with positive nucleic acid testing); (3) a history of contact with patients with fever or respiratory symptoms of the city of Wuhan and the surrounding areas, or other communities where cases of COVID-19 have been reported in the last 14 days prior to the appearance of the symptoms; (4) history of contact with groups of confirmed cases (-2 cases with fever or respiratory symptoms that occurred within 2 weeks in small areas, such as the house, the office, a school class, etc.).
Clinical manifestations: (1) fever or respiratory symptoms; (2) characteristics in images of COVID-19 infection; (3) total white blood cell counts showing a count of normal lymphocytes, diminished or reduced at the early onslaught.
Diagnosis of a confirmed case should be based on a suspicious case with some characteristic of pathogenic or serological evidence as follows: (1) positive polymerase chain reaction test positive for SARS-CoV-2; (2) sequential sequection of the complete viral genome that shows a high homogeneity to the renowned new coronavirus; (3) positive result for the specific IgM antibody and the specific IgG antibody against SARS-CoV-2 in a serum-pronged IgG antibody switch against SARS-CoV-2 from negative-to-negative to positive, or an increase of the title 4 times more in the recovery phase than in the acute phase.
We can see that real-time polymerase string reaction testing to detect nucleic acid in blood or airway samples was added to the second (January 18, 2020) and the third (January 22, 2020) edits.
The detection of pathogens of the blood samples was added at the fourth (January 27, 2020) and the fifth (8 February 2020) edits; and then the serological tests were added to the seventh edition.
- NS.
In addition, more and more evidence reminds us that we must have pr prudence with asymptomatic and atypical symptomatic and symptomatic patients.
Therefore, the flowchart of Zhou et al. should be upgraded, as they classified the person without clinical symptoms such as "low-threach."
The punctuation system should also be verified in other clinical studies and practices.
In conclusion, we hope that more direct evidence will emerge and we ask readers to send us their comments.
For the diagnosis of "suspect case" and "concretely," we recommend seeking and complying with the newest guidelines of their countries of origin.
Our team will also update our guideline in a timely manner to provide assistance.
Bangladesh announces five new deaths from COVID-19, the highest number in a day
Bangladesh yesterday confirmed five new fatalities from COVID-19 in one day.
It's the highest number of deaths in a day due to the virus.
Until yesterday, the Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control and Research (IEDCR) of Bangladesh reported that the number of infected registered cases included 114 active cases and 33 recovered cases that were staying in their homes.
There have been a total of 17 deaths.
At an online news briefing, IEDCR director Dr. Meerjady Sabrina Flora said the victims were four men and a woman.
According to Dr. Meerjady, two cases were more than 60 years of age, two between the ages of 51 and 60 to one between 41 and 50.
He also said that two of the victims were from Daca.
On 11 March the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 pandemic.
A hospital official told Anadolu Agency, a local news outlet that one of the dead was Jalal Saifur Rahman, director of the Anti-Corruption Commission of the Bangladesh Anti-Corruption Commission who was being cared for at the Kuwaiti Maitree Hospital.
OnSaturday, in an online video announcement, Bangladesh's Minister of Transport for Highways and Bridges, Obaidul Quader, said public transport would be suspended for longer than originally planned, until next Saturday.
This suspension of public transport had initially started on 26 March and was scheduled to end on Saturday, 4 April.
The transport of essential products such as medical supplies, fuel and food, was still permitted.
The first recorded cases of COVID-19 infection in Bangladesh occurred on March 8, two men who came back from Italy, as well as the wife.
By March 19, these three had already recovered.
More than a million SARS-CoV-2 Infections worldwide
On Thursday, the total number of SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus infections surpassed one million worldwide, according to Johns Hopkins University data.
At least 52,000 deaths were linked to COVID-19, the disease caused by the coronavirus.
The milestone took place on the same day Malaui confirmed its first coronavirus infections and Zambia had its first corona-virus-related death.
North Korea said on Thursday that it was one of the few countries without coronavirus infections.
Yesterday the World Health Organization reported 1 051 635 confirmed cases, including 79 332 cases in the twenty-four hours before 10:00 a.m., central European time (UTC-0800), on April 4.
In the U.S., there were more than 244,000 cases of coronavirus, linked to at least 5,900 deaths.
CBS News reported, quoting data from Johns Hopkins University that there were more than 1,000 deaths in the U.S. on Wednesday caused by coronavirus infections.
Around the world, countries announced stricter measures to inhibit the spread of the disease.
Sergei Sobyanin, the mayor of Moscow on Thursday, extended the city's closure until May 1.
At the national level, President Vladimir Putin stated that the Russians would continue to receive their salaries without going to work until April 30.
Portugal's parliament voted to extend the state of national emergency for 15 days; the vote was passed with 215 votes in favour, ten abstentions and one vote against.
Saudi Arabia extended the curfews in the holy cities of Mecca and Medina throughout the day; previously, the curfew was only maintained between 3:00 p.m. and 6 a.m.
Thailand planned to implement a curfew between 10:00 p.m. and 4:00 a.m.
Ohio Gov Gov. Mike DeWine announced that the state had extended its order to stay home until May 1.
Supermarkets in Australia lower limits on hygienic paper per transaction
On Sunday and Saturday afternoon, Australian supermarket chains Woolworths and Coles lowered their hygienic paper purchase limits to two and a package by transaction in all supermarkets in the country, respectively.
On Monday, ALDI also pushed the limit of a package.
These limitations were published as messages in the boxes and Facebook pages of the networks.
Consumers were reportedly grieddling for fear of COVID-19 in the event that people had to self-agent.
On Wednesday, Woolworths also confined purchases of toilet paper for home delivery to a package-by-price.
These changes were introduced after the previous four-petweety restriction per transaction implemented by Woolworths and Coles on March 4 and 5, respectively.
Coles, in its March 8 press release, reported that, with the four-bundle restriction app, "many supermarkets are still exhausting the product within an hour of delivery," and said the lawsuit "was unprecedented," while ALDI, in a Facebook post on Tuesday, said it was "unexpected."
Sales rose with a "strong increase" last week, according to a Woolworths spokeswoman for Woolworths.
The Costco supermarket in Canberra also stunned the amount to two packages last week.
To further alleviate the shortage, Coles requested larger packages from suppliers and increased the frequency of deliveries, Woolworths ordered additional stock, while ALDI made stocks available for a special offer on Wednesday planned in advance.
Russell Zimmerman, executive director of the Australian Retailers Association, said the retailers are trying to increase stocks, but local council restrictions on lorry delivery schedules make it difficult.
It foresees an increase in the costs of production, as suppliers try to meet the demand, and fewer offers.
On Tuesday, ALDI announced that, following the early release of stocks, some supermarkets are unable to make Wednesday's special offer.
In a report on News.com.au, Dr Gary Mortimer, a sales expert at the University of Technology of Queensland, said supermarkets supply stocks every night.
He stressed that hygienic paper is a voluminous item, which causes a low amount of stocks in numbers, and, when it runs out, leaves ample empty space on the shelves, which intensifies the sense of scarcity.
"Coles and Woolworths have the idea [that] if there's an plentiful amount of the item on the shelves, whether products like rolls of toilet paper and disinfectants could [buy] and there are large amounts, it would probably minimise panic," Russell Zimmerman told ABC News.
Recyctired toilet paper maker Who Gives a Crap said last Wednesday it depleted the stock.
Kimberly-Clark, which manufactures Kleenex hygienic paper, and Solaris Paper, which manufactures Sorbent, said they were working around the clock, seven days a week, to maintain supply, according to the report on news.com.au.
Domain.com, a real estate site, reported that some property sellers were offering free toilet paper to the first bidder at auction in Melbourne, when fewer auctions were held because buyers had time off the long Labor Day weekend.
Thursday's edition of NT News, a Darwin-printed diary, included an eight-story envelope designed to be cut and used as toilet paper.
Supermarkets at first were reluctant to impose restrictions, according to an ABC Australia report on March 3, in which they said they had no plans to introduce restrictions on purchases.
Russell Zimmerman added that other products also have plenty of in-demands, including masks, disinfectants, dry products, liquid soap and flour.
Similarly, outside of Australia on Sunday afternoon it was noticed that British online supermarket Ocado limited purchases of Andres hygienic paper to two 12-wall packages.
The World Health Organization declares COVID-19 pandemic
On Wednesday, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared the ongoing outbreak of COVID-19, the disease caused by the SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus.
Although the word "pandeemia" only refers to the magnitude of the spread of a disease, not how dangerous the specific cases are, WHO stressed the need to encourage governments to take action:
All countries can still change the course of this pandemic.
"If the countries detect, they do tests, treat, isolate, identify and mobilize their inhabitants in the response," said Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, director-general of the WHO.
We are very concerned, both about the alarming levels of propagation and severity, and the alarming levels of inaction.
According to Dr. Tom Frieden, a former director of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the pandemic is "unprecedented."
In statements published by CNN in February, he said, "In addition to the flu, no other respiratory virus has been traced from the onset to the continuing global propagation."
Ghebreyehis expressed a similar opinion, saying "we have never seen a pandemic caused by a coronavirus before."
"And we've never seen a pandemic that can be controlled at the same time at the same time," he added.
The new pandemic status emerges following the WHO's decision in January to declare the outbreak a public health emergency of international importance.
U.S. National Allergy and Ineducated Diseases Director of the U.S. Dr. Anthony Fauci said of the outbreak, "in conclusion, it's going to get worse."
As early as Thursday, the Associated Press reported that there were at least 12,000 COVID-19 cases worldwide, and more than 4,600 deaths.
The corona-virus pandemic 2019-20 is an ongoing coronavirus disease pandemic 2019 (COVID-19), Caused by Coronavirus 2 of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
The outbreak was identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019, it was declared an international public health emergency on January 30, 2020, and was recognized as a pandemic on March 11, 2020.
As of April 10, 2020, approximately 1.61 million cases of COVID-19 were reported in 210 countries and territories, and about 97,000 deaths as a result.
Around 364,000 people recovered.
It was estimated that the death rate per case rate is 4 per cent in China, while globally it ranges from 13.04 per cent in Algeria to 0.08 per cent in New Zealand.
Frequent symptoms include fever, coughing and shortness of breath.
Complications may include pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The time from exposure to the onset of symptoms is usually about five days, but it can vary from two to fourteen days.
There's no specific anti-viral vaccine or anti-viral treatment.
Primary treatment is symptomatic and supportive therapy. Recommended preventive measures include washing your hands, rubbing your mouth while coughing, staying distance from other people and self-isslaying and self-inseminating people who suspect they are infected.
Authorities around the world responded through the implementation of travel restrictions, quarantines, curfews, workplace hazard checks and facility closures.
The pandemic led to a serious global socio-economic alteration, the postponement or cancellation of sports, religious, political and cultural events, and the widespread shortage of supplies aggravated by panic-motivated purchases.
Schools and universities closed nationally or locally in 193 countries, affecting approximately 99.4 per cent of the world &apos; s student population.
Wrong information on the virus was disseminated on the Internet, and there were incidents of xenophobia and discrimination against Chinese people, others of Eastern and south-eastern Asian descent and south-eastern appearance, and others from areas with significant virus cases.
Due to the reduction of the travel and the closures of the heavy industry, there was a decrease in air pollution and carbon emissions.
On December 31, 2019, the health authorities in Wuhan, China (the capital of Hubei province) reported cases of pneumonia of unknown cause, and in early January 2020 an investigation was initiated.
The cases, for the most part, were related to Huanan's seafood wholesale market, so it was thought that the virus had a Zonotic origin.
The virus that caused the outbreak is known as SARS-CoV-2, a newly undiscovered virus closely linked to bat coronaviruses, pangolin coronaviruses and SARS-CoV. Later, it was discovered that the first person who was known to show symptoms fell ill on December 1, 2019, and that person did not have visible connections to the last wet market group.
Of the first group of cases reported in December 2019, two-thirds were found to have a link to the market.
On March 13, 2020, an unverifiable report by the South China Morning Post suggested that a case dating back to November 17, 2019 of a 55-year-old from Hubei province could have been the first. On February 26, 2020, the WHO reported that, as the new cases reportedly decreased in China but suddenly increased in Italy, Iran and South Korea, the number of new cases outside China had for the first time surpassed the number of new cases within China.
There may be a considerable number of uninformed cases, especially among those with milder symptoms.
As at February 26, relatively few cases had been reported in young people, with those aged 19 and under 2.4 per cent accounted for 2.4 per cent of cases around the world. Patrick Vallance, the United Kingdom &apos; s chief scientific adviser, estimated that 60 per cent of the British population would have to become infected before effective collective immunity could be achieved.
The cases refer to the number of people who were tested for COVID-19, and who obtained a positive result confirmed according to the official protocols.
As of 23 March, no country had carried out tests in more than 3 per cent of its population, and many countries have had official non-testing policies on people who only had mild symptoms, such as Italy, the Netherlands, Spain and Switzerland.
A study published on March 16 revealed that in China, up to 23 January, an estimated 86 per cent of COVID-19 infections had not been detected, and these unrecorded infections were 79 per cent of recorded sources of infection.
A statistical analysis published on March 30 estimated that the amounts of infections in Italy were significantly higher than reported cases.
Initial estimates of the basic reproductive number (R0) for COVID-19 were 1.4 to 2.4.
A study published by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention found it could be 5.7.
Most people with COVID-19 are recovering.
For those who don't, the time elapsed from the development of symptoms to death has been between 6 and 41 days, most often 14 days.
As at 10 April 2020, approximately 97,000 deaths had been attributed to COVID-19.
In China, as of February 5, about 80 per cent of the deaths accounted for people over the age of 60, and 75 per cent had pre-existing diseases, including cardiovascular diseases and diabetes. Official records of deaths from the COVID-19 pandemic generally refer to the people who tested positive in the COVID test, according to official protocols.
It is possible that the number of actual deaths due to COVID-19 is much higher, as it may not include the people who died without the test, for example in their homes, in nursing homes, etc.
Participal data from Italy revealed that the number of additional deaths during the pandemic exceeded the official record of COVID deaths at a factor 4 to 5 times.
A spokeswoman for the U.S. U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) admitted "We know [the number of deaths declared] is an underestimate," a statement corroborated by the anecdotal sub-count reports in the U.S. This underestimation is common in pandemics, such as in the H1N1 swine flu pandemic of 2009. The first confirmed death occurred in Wuhan on January 9, 2020.
The first death outside mainland China occurred on February 1 in the Philippines, and the first death outside of Asia occurred in France on February 14.
More than a dozen deaths were recorded outside mainland China on February 28, more than a dozen deaths were recorded in each of the countries of Iran, South Korea and Italy.
As at 13 March, more than forty countries and territories had reported deaths on all continents except Antarctica. Various indicators are usually used to quantify mortality.
These numbers vary according to the region and over time, and are influenced by the quantity of tests, the quality of the health system, the treatment options, the time elapsed since the initial outbreak and the characteristics of the population, such as age, sex and general health. The proportion of case deaths reflects the number of deaths diagnosed by the number of cases diagnosed within a certain time interval.
According to Johns Hopkins University statistics, the global share of case deaths is 6.0 per cent (97 039/1 617 204) as at April 10, 2020.
The number varies according to the region.
In China, estimates of the proportion of case deaths decreased from 17.3 per per cent (in those with symptoms from 1 to 10 January 2020) to 0.7 per cent (in those with symptoms after February 1, 2020). Other indicators include the rate of fatality in cases, which reflects the percentage of people diagnosed who died from a disease, and the rate of letality due to infections, which reflects the percentage of infected (diagnosed and undiagnosed) who died from a disease.
These stats do not have a certain period of time and consider a specific population from the infection to the resolution of the case.
Some academic academics have tried to calculate these numbers for specific populations.
The Centre for Evidence-Based Medicine at the University of the University of Oxford estimates the rate of lethality for pandemic infections as a whole is between 0.1 per cent and 0.39 per cent.
The superior estimate of this range is consistent with the results of the first random tests for COVID-19 in Germany, and a statistical study that analyzed the impact of the tests on estimates of the lethality rate per case.
The WHO states that the pandemic can be controlled.
Peak and the final duration of the bud are uncertain and may differ depending on the location.
Maciej Boni of the Pennsylvania State University said: "Infectious outbreaks, if they aren't controlled, are generally stabilized and then begin to decrease when the disease runs out of available hostesses.
But at the moment it's almost impossible to make any reasonable foresight of when that will happen.'
The main medical adviser of the Chinese government, Zhong Nanshan, said it "would be completed in June" if all countries can be mobilized to follow the WHO recommendation on measures to stop the spread of the virus.
Adam Kucharski of the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine on March 17 said SARS-CoV-2 "would be circulating, possibly for one or two years."
According to Neil Ferguson &apos; s-educated Imperial College study, physical distancing and other measures will be necessary "until a vaccine (possibly 18 months or more) is available."
William Schaffner of Vanderbilt University stated, "I think it is unlikely that this coronavirus, since it is transmitted so easily, will disappear altogether" and "could become a seasonal disease that reappears every year."
The virulence of the reappearance would depend on collective immunity and the magnitude of the mutation.
Symptoms of COVID-19 can be relatively non-sensual and infected people can be asymptomatic.
The two most common symptoms are fever (88 per cent) and dry cough (68 per cent).
Less common symptoms include fatigue, shoe production in the air tract (fleme), loss of smell, lack of air, muscle and joint pain, sore throats, headache, chills, vomiting, hemoptysis, diarrhea or cyanosis. WHO states that approximately one in six people become seriously ill and has difficulty breathing.
According to the U.S. U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), emergency symptoms include difficulty breathing, pressure or lingering chest pain, sudden confusion, difficulty waking and bluish color on the face or clenchedness of the face or lips; at the manifestation of these symptoms, immediate medical attention is recommended. The increased progression of the disease can cause severe pneumonia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, sepsis, septic shock and death.
Some of the infected people may be asymptomatic, don't experience clinical symptoms, even if the test results confirm the infection, so the researchers advised to carefully control and examine those who have close contact with confirmed infected people in order to rule out the infection.
China's estimates of asymptomatic people ostensibly ostensibly ostensibly to 44 per cent.
The usual incubation period (the time between the infection and the onset of symptoms) ranges from one to 14 days; more frequently it is 5 days. As an example of the uncertainty, the estimate of the proportion of people with COVID-19 who lost their sense of smell at the beginning was 30 per cent and then decreased to 15 per cent.
Some details of how the disease spreads are still being determined.
It is believed that the disease spreads mainly during close contact and through the small droplets that occur when coughing, sneezing or talking; close contact is considered a distance of 1 to 2 meters (3 to 6 feet).
According to the studies, by coughing uncovered, the droplets can move from 4.5 meters (15 feet) to 8.2 meters (27 feet).
Some have proposed that the virus can also be transmitted through small droplets that remain in the air for longer periods, which can occur when speaking. Respiratory droplets can also occur during exhalation, even when talking, although the virus is usually not transmitted through the air.
Dicklets can go into the mouth or nose of the people who are nearby or possibly inhale to the lungs.
Some medical procedures, such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), may cause the aerosolization of respiratory secretions and, therefore, aerial propagation.
It can also propagate when one touches a polluted surface, including the skin, and then touches the eyes, nose or mouth.
While there's a concern that it can be transmitted through stool, it's believed that this risk is low.
The Government of China denied the possibility of SARS-CoV-2 oral faecal transmission. The virus is more contagious during the first three days after the onslaught of symptoms, although the spread may be possible before symptoms are manifested and in more advanced stages of the disease.
People tested positive for the disease up to three days before the onset of symptoms, indicating that transmission is possible before developing significant symptoms.
There's only a few laboratory-confized asymptomatic case reports, but some countries have identified asymptomatic transmission during contact tracing investigations.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) argues that, although it is not entirely clear the ease with which the disease spreads, a person usually infects two or three others. The virus survives from hours to days on the surfaces.
Specifically, it has been determined that the virus is detectable for up to three days in plastic (polypropylene) and 304 stainless steel, one day in cardboard and for up to four hours in copper.
This, however, varies according to humidity and temperature. Positive results were obtained in COVID-19 tests performed on pets and other animals.
There's no evidence that the animals can transmit the virus to humans, although the British authorities recommend washing their hands after contact with animals, as well as after contact with other surfaces they could have touched infected people.
Coronavirus 2 of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a new virus that first isolated from three people with pneumonia linked to a group of cases of acute respiratory disease in Wuhan.
All characteristics of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus are found in the related coronaviruses in nature. Outside the human body, the virus dies upon coming into contact with soap, which dissolves its protective wrapping. The SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-CoV.
He is believed to have a Zonotic origin.
Genetic analyses revealed that the coronavirus is genetically grouped with the genus betacoronavirus, its subgender sarbecovirus (branage B) along with two strains derived from the bat.
At the full genome level, it is 96 percent identical to other bat coronavirus samples (BatCov RaTG13).
In February 2020, Chinese researchers discovered that there is only one difference in amino acids in certain parts of genome sequentials between pangolines viruses and those of humans.
To date, the comparison of the entire genome has indicated that pangolines coronavirus and SARS-CoV-2 share at most 92 percent of the genetic material, which is not enough to demonstrate that pangolines are the intermediate host.
Infection of the virus can be provisionally diagnosed on the basis of symptoms, although it is ultimately confirmed through the polymerase chain reaction with reverse transcriptase (rRT-PCR) of infected secretions or by CT scan imaging.
According to a study carried out in Wuhan in which polymerase string reaction was compared with CT scan, CT scans is much more sensitive than polymerase chain reaction, although less specific, and many of the characteristics in the images coincide with the processes of other diseases and pneumonia.
Since March 2020, the U.S. College of Radiology has recommended "not using CT scans as a method of detection or a front-line test for diagnosing COVID-19."
The WHO has published several RNA test protocols for SARS-CoV-2, the first published on January 17.
The test uses the real-time reverse polymerase string reaction (rRT-PCR).
The test can be done on blood or respiratory samples.
The results are usually available within a few hours or days.
Usually, this test is carried out with a nasopharyngeal hyssop, although you can also use a throat slope. Some laboratories and companies are developing serological tests that detect antibodies.
As at April 6, 2020, none of these proved to be precise enough to approve their widespread use.
In the U.S., a serological test developed by Cellex was approved for emergency use by certified labs only.
Characteristic traits in imaging images and CT CT sympathises include opacities in asymmetric peripheral delustrated glass and absent pleural spills.
The Italian Society of Radiology is compiling an international online database of the results obtained from confirmed case images.
Due to the overlap with other infections such as adenovirus, unconfirmed imaging imaging via polymerase chain reaction has a limited specificity in the identification of COVID-19.
A wide-ranging study carried out in China compared the results of CT shootered shades with those of polymerase chain reaction and showed that, although imaging diagnosis is less specific to infection, it is faster and more sensitive, suggesting its consideration as a detection tool in areas affected by the epidemic.
Convolutional neural networks based on artificial intelligence were developed to detect characteristics of the virus in the images, both with X-rays and CT scans.
Strategies to prevent the transmission of the disease include maintaining good general personal hygiene, washing your hands, avoiding touching your eyes, nose or mouth with your hands without washing them first, sneezing or crawling on a disposable handkerchief and throwing the handkerchief directly into a waste container.
It is not recommended that those who may already be infected wear a surgical mask in public.
Physical distancing measures are also recommended to prevent the transmission. Many Governments have restricted or ill-obsessed all non-essential journeys to and from countries and areas affected by the outbreak.
The virus, however, has reached the stage of community spread in many parts of the world.
This means that the virus is spreading within the communities, and some members of the community don't know where or how they became infected. It's recommended that health-care providers who serve someone who might be infected take standard precautions, contact precautions and use eye protection. Tracing contacts is an important method for health authorities to determine the origin of an infection and to prevent further transmission.
The government's use of mobile phone location data for this purpose has raised concerns about privacy, and Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations issued a statement demanding limits on this type of surveillance.
Several voluntary mobile applications have been implemented or proposed, and as of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen expert groups were working on Privacy Resculine Solutions, such as using Bluetooth to record a user's proximity to other cell phones.
Users then receive a message if they were in close contact with someone who tested positive for COVID-19. Misconceptions are circulating about how to prevent the infection; e.g. nasal washing and gargarbages with oral rins are not effective.
There isn't a COVID-19 vaccine, although so many organizations are working to develop one.
It is recommended hand-washing to prevent the spread of the disease.
The CDC recommends that people waslated their hands frequently with soap and water for at least twenty seconds, especially after using the bathroom or when their hands are viscerally dirty; before eating and smiling their nose, coughing or sneezing.
This is because, outside the human body, the virus dies when it comes into contact with soap, which disintegrates its protective bubble.
The CDC also recommended using an alcohol-based hand disinfectant with at least 60 per cent alcohol by volume when soap and water are not available.
The WHO advises people to avoid touching their eyes, nose or mouth with their hands without washing them first.
The surfaces can be decontaminated with several solutions (within one minute after exposure to disinfectant on a stainless steel surface), including ethanol at 62-71 per cent, isopropanol at 50-100 per cent, 0.1 per cent sodium hypochlorite at 0.1 per cent, hydrogen peroxide at 0.5 per cent and povidone iodated at 0.2-7.5 per cent.
Other solutions such as benzalconium chloride and chlorhexidine gluconate are less effective.
The CDC recommends that, if a COVID case is suspected or confirmed in a facility, such as an office or nursery, all areas such as offices such as offices, bathrooms, shared electronic spaces, such as tablets, touch screens, keyboards, remote controls and Autonomous ATMs are disinfected.
The health organizations recommend that people cover their mouth and nose with their elbow flexes or with a disposable handkerchief to cough or sneeze, and discard the handkerchief immediately.
It is recommended that those who may be infected will wear surgical masks, since the use of a mask can limit the volume and distance traveled by respiratory droplets that disperse when talking, sneezing and crawling.
The WHO issued instructions on when and how to wear the masks.
According to Stephen Griffin, a virologist at the University of Leeds, "Using a mask can reduce the tendency [for] people to touch their faces, which is an important source of infection without proper hand hygiene." It has also been recommended to wear masks in people caring for someone who might have the disease.
The WHO has recommended the use of masks by healthy people only if they are at a high risk, such as those caring for a person with COVID-19, although it also recognizes that wearing masks can help people avoid touching their faces.
A number of countries have begun to encourage the use of masks by the public.
In the U.S., the CDC recommends wearing non-meank fabric-produced masks. China specifically recommended the use of disposable medical masks by healthy members of the public, especially when they're in close contact (1 meter (3 feet) or less with other people.
Hong Kong recommends using a surgical mask to take public transport or be in busy places.
Health officials in Thailand advise people to wear cloth masks in their homes and wash them on a daily basis.
The Czech Republic and Slovakia banned from going out to public spaces without wearing a mask or without rubbing its nose and mouth.
On March 16, Vietnam called for all people to wear a mask because they are in public spaces to be able to protect themselves and protect others.
The Government of Austria demanded the use of masks by all persons entering a supermarket.
Israel called on all residents to wear masks in public.
Taiwan, which has manufactured 10 million masks per day since mid-March, on April 1, demanded the use of masks by passengers on intercity trains and buses.
Panama imposed the mandatory use of masks to go outdoors, while it also recommends the manufacture of homemade masks to those who can't buy them.
Masks have also been widely used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia and Singapore.
Social estrangement (also known as physical estrangement) includes infection control measures designed to stop the spread of the disease by minimizing close contact between people.
Methods include quarantines, travel restrictions and the closure of schools, workplaces, stadiums, theaters and shopping malls.
Individuals can implement socially distancing methods by staying in their homes, limiting travel, avoiding busy areas, using forms of greeting that don't include contact and physically distancing themselves from other people.
Many governments now demand or recommend social estrangement in regions affected by the outbreak.
The maximum size of meetings recommended by U.S. government agencies and health organizations fell rapidly from 250 people (if there was no known spread of COVID-19 in a region) to 50 people, and then to 10 people.
On March 22, 2020, Germany banned public meetings of more than two. Older adults and those with underlying diseases such as diabetes, heart disease, respiratory diseases, hypertension and committed immune systems are at an increased risk for serious diseases and complications, and the CDC recommends that they stay in their homes as much as possible in the areas of the community affected by the outbreak. At the end of March 2020, WHO and other health agencies began to replace the use of the term "distance" with "physics distance," to clarify that the objective is to reduce physical contact while maintaining social connections, either virtually or remotely.
The use of the term "social distance" has relianceed the assumption that people should adopt complete social isolation, rather than encourage them to stay in touch with other people through alternative means. Some authorities have published guidelines on sexual health to use during the pandemic.
They include recommendations to only have sexual intercourse with a person with whom he is alive, who does not have the virus or symptoms of the virus.
It was recommended the self-isolation at the home of those who were diagnosed with COVID-19 and those who are suspected to have become infected.
Health agencies have issued detailed instructions on proper self-isolation. Many governments have demanded or recommended the self-squaring of entire populations in the affected areas.
Stricter self-care instructions were issued for those belonging to high-risk groups.
Persons who may have been in contact with someone with COVID-19 and who have recently travelled to a country or region with widespread transmission are advised to do self-aquarency for 14 days from the time of the last possible exposure.
The strategies to control an outbreak are containment or suppression and mitigation.
Containment is carried out in the early stages of the outbreak and aims to identify and isolate infected persons, in addition to introducing other infection control measures and vaccines to prevent the disease from spreading to the rest of the population.
When it is no longer possible to control the spread of the disease, efforts are moved to the mitigation phase: measures are taken to curb the spread and mitigate its effects on the health system and society.
A combination of containment and mitigation measures can be adopted at the same time.
Removal requires more extreme measures to reverse the pandemic through the reduction of basic reproductive numbers to less than 1. Part of controlling the outbreak of an infectious disease involves trying to reduce the epidemic spike, which is known as flattening the curve of the epidemic.
This decreases the risk of health services collapsing and allows more time for the development of vaccines and treatments.
Non-pharmaceutical interventions that can control the outbreak include personal preventative measures, such as hand hygiene, the use of masks and self-sale; community measures aimed at physical distancing, such as the closure of schools and the cancellation of mass concentury events; the Community commitment to promote acceptance and participation in such interventions, as well as environmental measures and area cleaning. In China, more drastic measures were taken to contain the outbreak once its severity became evident, for example, the implementation of quarantines in entire cities and the imposition of strict travel bans.
Other countries also took a variety of measures aimed at limiting the spread of the virus.
South Korea implemented mass-detection assessments and localized quarantines, and issued warnings about the movements of infected people.
Singapore provided financial support to infected people who decided to quarantine and imposed heavy fines on those who did not.
Taiwan increased the production of masks and sanctioned the accumulation of medical supplies. Simulations in Great Britain and the United States show that mitigation (decreasing the spread of the epidemic but doesn't stop it) and suppression (reversing the growth of the pandemic) have great challenges.
Optimal mitigation policies could reduce maximum demand for health care by two-thirds and deaths by half, but still generate hundreds of thousands of deaths and the collapse of health systems.
Repeat may be preferred, but it must be maintained for as long as the virus circulates in the human population (or until there is a vaccine available, if that happens first), otherwise the transmission is quickly reactivated when the measurements become more flexible.
Long-term intervention to eliminate the pandemic implies social and economic costs.
There are no specific anti-viral medicines approved for COVID-19, but efforts are being made to develop them, which includes testing existing drugs.
Taking over-the-counter medicines for the cold, drinking fluids, and resting can help relieve the symptoms.
Depending on the severity, oxygenotherapy, intravenous fluids and respiratory assistance may be needed.
Using steroids can worsen outcomes.
A number of compounds that have previously been approved for the treatment of other viral diseases are being investigated for use in VOVID-19 treatment.
The WHO also indicated that some "homereal and traditional remedies" can alleviate symptoms caused by SARS-CoV-19.
The WHO describes the increase in capacity and the adaptation of health care to the needs of patients with COVID-19 as a key response to the outbreak.
ECDC and the WHO Regional Office for Europe have developed guidelines for hospitals and primary health-care services for the redistribution of resources at various levels, which includes focusing laboratory services on COVID-19 tests, canceling optional procedures where possible, separating and insulating positive patients in COVID-19 and increasing intensive care skills through staff training and increasing the number of respirators and beds available.
There are several theories about where the first case (the so-called Zero patient) may have originated.
The first known case of the new coronavirus can be traced back to December 1, 2019 in Wuhan, Hubei, China.
Over the course of a month the number of coronavirus cases in Hubei gradually increased.
These were linked mainly to Huanan &apos; sarcomestic seafood market, which was also selling live animals, and one theory is that the virus came from one of these animals; or, in other words, it has a Zonotic Origin. On December 26, a series of cases of pneumonia of unknown causes were observed for which Dr. Zhang Jixian was cared at Hubei Provincial Hospital, who informed the Jianghan CDC of Wuhan on December 27.
On December 30, a group of doctors at Wuhan's Central Hospital warned their colleagues about an "FSRS-like coronavirus."
Police admonished eight of these doctors, including Li Wenliang, for spreading fake rumours, and another doctor, Ai Fen, was reprimanded by her superiors for raising the alarm.
Later, on 31 December the Wuhan Municipal Health Commission issued a public note and informed WHO.
As at the beginning of January, sufficient cases of unfamiliar pneumonia had been reported to health authorities in Wuhan to initiate an investigation. During the early stages of the outbreak, the number of cases doubled approximately every seven-and-a-a-half days.
At the beginning and mid-January of 2020, the virus spread to other Chinese provinces, contributing migration through the Chinese New Year and the fact that Wuhan is a transport hub and an important rail crossing.
On January 20, China reported about 140 new cases in one day, including two people in Beijing and one in Shenzhen.
Previous official data show that, as of 20 January 2020, 6174 people had already developed symptoms.As of March 26, the U.S. has surpassed China and Italy with the highest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, more than 1.61 million cases have been reported around the world; more than 77,000 people have died and more than 364 thousand have recovered.
Around 200 countries and territories have had at least one case.
Due to the pandemic in Europe, many countries in the Schengen area have restricted free movement and established border controls.
National reactions have included containment measures, such as quarantines (known as orders to stay at home, orders of shelter on site or confinement) and curfews. As at 2 April, about 300 million people, or approximately 90 per cent of the population, meet some form of confinement in the United States, more than 50 million people are confined in the Philippines, some 59 million people are confined in South Africa and 1.3 billion people are confined in India.
As at March 26, 1.7 billion people around the world complied with some form of containment, rising to 2.6 billion people two days later, about a third of the world's population.
COVID-19's first confirmed case dates back to December 1, 2019 in Wuhan; an unconfirmed report indicates the first case occurred on November 17.
On December 26, Dr. Zhang Jixian observed a group of cases of unfamiliar cause pneumonia, and her hospital informed the CDC of Jianghan of Wuhan on December 27.
The first genetic tests of patient samples on December 27, 2019 indicated the presence of an SARS-like coronavirus.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Commission issued a public note on December 31.
WHO was briefed on the same day.
While these notifications were being made, the police warned the doctors in Wuhan for "dispossessing rumours" about the outbreak.
China's National Health Commission initially said there was "not clear evidence" of human transmission.
At the end of January, the Chinese government launched a scathing campaign, later described by Chinese Communist Party Secretary-General Xi Jinping as a "people's war," to curb the spread of the virus.
In what was described as "the greatest quarantine in the history of humanity," on 23 January a health cordon was announced prohibiting travel inside and outside Wuhan, this spread to a total of 15 cities in Hubei, and affected a total of about 57 million people.
The use of private vehicles in the city was banned.
Chinese New New Year celebrations (25 January) were canceled in many places.
The authorities also announced the construction of a temporary hospital, Huoshenshan Hospital, which was completed in 10 days.
Subsequently, another hospital, Leishenshan Hospital, was renovated to receive more patients.
In addition to the new hospitals built, China also converted another 14 facilities in Wuhan, such as convention centers and stadiums, into temporary hospitals. On January 26, the Government implemented more measures to contain the COVID-19 outbreak, which included the issuance of health declarations for travelers and the extension of the Spring Feast Holiday.
Universities and schools were also closed across the country.
The regions of Hong Kong and Macau implemented a number of measures, especially in relation to schools and universities.
Remote working measures have been put in place in various regions of China.
Jourist restrictions were implemented inside and outside Hubei.
Public transport was modified and museums were temporarily closed throughout China.
The control of the public movement was implemented in several cities, and it has been estimated that about 760 million people (more than half of the population) faced some kind of restriction on outdoor outdoor exits. After the start of the global phase of the outbreak in March, the Chinese authorities took strict measures to prevent the "importation" of the virus from other countries.
For example, Beijing has imposed a mandatory quarantine of 14 days for all international travelers entering the city. As of March 23, in mainland China there had been only one case had been transmitted at the national level in the previous five days, on this occasion through a traveler who returned from Istanbul to Canton.
On March 24, 2020, Chinese Prime Minister Li Keqiang reported that the spread of cases transmitted at the national level was basically blocked and that the outbreak had been controlled in China.
On the same day, travel restrictions were relaxed in Hubei, as well as in Wuhan, two months after confinement was imposed. On March 26, 2020, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of China announced that the entry of visa holders or the residence permit would be suspended from March 28, with no specific details of when this policy will be completed.
Those who wish to enter China must apply for visas at Chinese embassies or consulates.
The Government of China pushed the companies and factories to reopen on March 30 and provided economic stimulus packages for the firms. The State Council declared a day of mourning that it will begin with a three-minute moment of national silence on April 4 at 10 a.m., coinciding with the Qingming Festival, although the central government asked the families to pay their respects online to comply with the physical disdain in order to prevent the resurgence of COVID-19.
On January 20, 2020, it was confirmed that the COVID-19 spread from China to South Korea.
On February 20, the country &apos; s health agency reported a significant increase in confirmed cases, attributed mainly to a meeting in Daegu of a new religious movement known as the Church of Jesus Shincheonji.
Shincheonji devotees who visited Daegu from Wuhan are suspected to be the source of the outbreak.
As of February 22, of the church's 9336 followers, 12.61 or about 13 per cent reported symptoms. On February 23, 2020, South Korea declared the highest alert level.
On February 28, there were more than 2,000 confirmed cases in Korea, which increased to 3150 on 29 February.
South Korea's military bases were quarantined after tests confirmed that three soldiers tested positive for the virus.
The schedules of the airlines were also affected, and were therefore modified. South Korea presented what was considered the largest and best-organized program in the world to carry out virus detection tests in the population and isolate infected people, in addition to identifying and quarantine those who were in contact with them.
Detection methods included the mandatory self-report of symptoms by newly arrived people from other countries through a mobile app, drive-thru centres to conduct virus detection tests with results available the following day and the increase in diagnostic capacity for up to 20,000 people every day to be able to test in up to 20,000 people every day.
South Korea &apos; s program is seen as a success in controlling the outbreak despite not adopting quarantine in entire cities. South Korea's society was initially polarized about President Moon Jae-in &apos; s response to the crisis.
Many North Koreans signed petitions to demand Moon impeachment for what they considered a mismanagement of the outbreak by the government, or to praising his response.
On March 23, it was reported that South Korea had the total number of cases on a lower day in four weeks.
On March 29, it was reported that, from 1 April, all newly arrived persons from abroad must be quarantined for two weeks.
According to media reports on April 1, South Korea received requests for support for the testing of the virus from 121 different countries.
On February 19, Iran reported its first confirmed cases of SARS-CoV-2 infections in Qom, where, according to the Ministry of Health and Medical Education, two people died later the same day.
Early measures announced by the government included the cancellation of concerts and other cultural events, sporting events and Friday's prayers and the closure of universities, higher education institutions and schools.
Iran allocated five billion riys to fight the virus.
President Hassan Rouhani said there were no plans to quarantine the areas affected by the outbreak on February 26, and that people would only be quarantined.
In March, plans were announced to limit travel between the cities, although heavy traffic between the cities as a result of the Persian Nowruz new year continued.
Shiite shrines in Qom remained open to pilgrims until March 16, 2020. In February, Iran became a center for the spread of the virus after China.
Amid claims of a cover-up over the scale of the outbreak in Iran, more than 10 countries had traced their cases to Iran by February 28, indicating that the magnitude of the outbreak could be more serious than the 388 cases reported by the Iranian government by that date.
The Iranian parliament was closed, and on March 3, 23 of its 290 members reported that they obtained positive results in the virus test.
On March 12, Human Rights Watch urged the Iranian prison authorities to unconditionally liberate human rights defenders detained for peaceful dissent, and also to temporarily release all eligible prisoners.
He pointed out that there is a greater risk of the spread of the virus in closed institutions, such as detention centres, which also lack adequate medical care.
On March 15, the Iranian government reported 100 fatalities on the same same day, the largest number recorded in the country since the beginning of the outbreak.
As at March 17, at least 12 Iranian ex-politicians or practising politicians had died from the disease.
As of March 23, Iran experienced 50 new cases per hour and a new death every ten minutes due to the coronavirus.
According to a WHO official, there may be five times as many cases in Iran than are reported.
It's also suggested that U.S. U.S. sanctions on Iran could be affecting the country's financial capacity to respond to the viral outbreak.
The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights has called for the relief of economic sanctions for the countries most affected by the pandemic, including Iran.
On January 31, it was confirmed the spread of the outbreak to Italy, when two Chinese tourists tested positive for SARS-CoV-2 tests in Rome.
Cases began to escalate rapidly, prompting the Italian government to suspend all flights to and from China and to declare a state of emergency.
Subsequently, an unrelated COVID-19 case group was detected, which began with 16 confirmed cases in Lombardy on February 21. On February 22, the Council of Ministers announced a new decree-law to contain the outbreak, which included quarantine more than 50,000 people from 11 different municipalities in northern Italy.
Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte said: "You won't be allowed to get in or out of areas affected by the outbreak.
The suspension of labor activities and sporting events in these areas has already been ordered." On March 4, the Italian government ordered the complete closure of schools and universities across the country while Italy reached 100 deaths.
All major sporting events, including Serie A soccer matches, had to take place behind closed doors until April, but on March 9, all sporting events were completely suspended for at least a month.
On March 11, Prime Minister Conte ordered the interruption of virtually all commercial activities except supermarkets and pharmacies. On March 6, the Italian College of Anesthetics, Analgesia, Resuscitation and Intensive Care (SIAARTI) published medical ethics recommendations on triage protocols that could be used.
On March 19, Italy surpassed China as the country with the largest number of corona-virus-related deaths in the world after reporting 3405 deaths from the pandemic.
On March 22, it was reported that Russia had dispatched nine military aircraft with medical equipment to Italy.
As of 5 April, there were 128 948 confirmed cases, 15 887 deaths and 21 815 recoveries in Italy, and the majority of these cases occurred in the Lombardy region.
A CNN report indicated that Italy's combination of a large elderly population and the inability to test all people who have the virus so far could contribute to the high rate of lethality.
The United Kingdom &apos; s response to the virus first arose as one of the most relaxed affected countries, and until 18 March 2020, the British Government did not impose any measures of social estrangement or massive quarantine on its citizens.
As a result, the Government was criticised for the apparent lack of speed and intensity in its response to concerns faced by the public. On March 16, Prime Minister Boris Johnson made an announcement in which he discouraged social contact and non-essential travel, and suggested that people work from their homes when possible and avoid places such as bars, restaurants and theaters.
On March 20, the Government announced the closure as soon as possible of all leisure establishments, such as bars and gyms, and promised to pay up to 80 per cent of workers &apos; wages up to a limit of GBP 2500 per month to prevent unemployment during the crisis.On 23 March, the Prime Minister announced stricter social estrangement measures, which prohibited meetings of more than two people and restricted outdoor travel and activities to those deemed strictly necessary.
Contrary to previous measures, the police could demand compliance with these restrictions through the imposition of fines and the dispersion of meetings.
It ordered the closure of the majority of the companies, with the exception of those considered "essential," including supermarkets, pharmacies, banks, hardware stores, gas stations and workshops.
On January 20, the first known case of COVID-19 was confirmed in Washington state north-west of the U.S. in a man who had returned from Wuhan on January 15.
On January 29, the White House's Coronavirus working group was established.
On January 31, the Trump administration declared a public health emergency and imposed restrictions on the entry of travelers from China.
On January 28, 2020, the Center for Disease Control and Prevention, the U.S. government's main public health institute, announced they had developed their own test kit.
Despite doing so, the U.S. had a sluggish start to carry out tests, which concealed the true magnitude of the outbreak at the time.
Testing was tarnished by defective test kits produced by the federal government in February, a lack of approval by the federal government for non-governmental test kits (from academies, companies and hospitals) until the end of February and the restrictive criteria for people qualifies for a test until early in March (then a medical order was required).
As of February 27, the Washington Post reported that less than 4,000 tests had been conducted in the U.S.
As of March 13, The Atlantic reported fewer than 14,000 tests had been carried out.
On March 22, The Associated Press reported: "Many people who have symptoms and a medical warrant have waited hours or days for a test." After the first U.S. death in the U.S. was reported on February 29 in the state of Washington, Gov Gov. Jay Inslee declared a state of emergency, a measure soon followed by other states.
Schools in the Seattle area canceled classes on March 3, and by mid-March, they closed schools across the country. March 6, 2020, a group of epidemiologists from Imperial College London informed the United States of forecasts for the impact of the new coronavirus in the country.
On the same day, President Trump signed the Supplemental Assemblies for Coronavirus Preparing and Answer Act, which allocated $8.3 billion in emergency funding to federal agencies to respond to the outbreak.
The corporations imposed travel restrictions on employees, canceled conferences and motivated employees to work from home.
Seasons and sporting events were canceled. On March 11, Trump announced travel restrictions to most countries in Europe except the United Kingdom for 30 days, effective March 13.
The next day, he extended the restrictions to include the United Kingdom and Ireland.
On March 13, he declared the national emergency, making federal funds available to respond to the crisis.
Starting March 15, many companies closed or reduced their hours across the U.S. to try to reduce the spread of the virus.
As of March 17, the epidemic had been confirmed in all 50 states and the District of Columbia. On March 23, it was reported that New York City had 10 700 cases of coronavirus, more than the total number of cases in South Korea.
On March 25, the governor said the social estrangement appeared to be in place, as estimates of the duplication of cases went from two days to 4.7 days.
As of March 28, there were 32 308 confirmed cases in New York City, and 672 people had died from the virus. On March 26, it was reported that the U.S. had more confirmed coronavirus infections than any other country in the world, including China and Italy. As of April 8, 400 335 cases had been confirmed in the U.S. and 12,841 people had died.
U.S. U.S. President Trump decided to extend the social estrangement guidelines until April 30, according to media reports on March 30.
On the same day he anchored the USNS Comfort in New York, a hospital boat with about 1,000 beds.
On April 3, the U.S. had a record of 884 deaths within a 24-hour period due to coronavirus.
On April 3 in New York State, cases surpassed 100,000. The White House has been criticized for underestimating the threat and controlling the messages by telling health officials and scientists to co-ordinate public statements and virus-related publications with Vice President Mike Pence.
The blanket endorsement of Trump's handling of the crisis has been polarized between partisan lines.
Some U.S. officials and commentators criticized U.S. U.S. reliance on importing critical materials, including special medical supplies from China.
An analysis of the travel patterns was used to identify and predict the patterns of propagation and was published in The Journal of Travel Medicine in mid-January 2020.
Based on 2018 information from the International Air Transport Association, Bangkok, Hong Kong, Tokyo and Taipei had as many travelers since Wuhan.
Dubai, Sydney and Melbourne were also identified as popular destinations for people travelling from Wuhan.
Bali was reported to be the least capable city among the 20 most popular destination cities in terms of preparation, while Australia's cities were considered the most enjoyable. On February 7, Australia published its Emergency Response Plan to the New Coronavirus (COVID-19).
He said a lot had yet to be unveiled about COVID-19, and that Australia would emphasize border control and communications in its response to the pandemic.
A human biosafety emergency was declared in Australia on March 21.
Due to the effective quarantine of public transport in Wuhan and Hubei, several countries have planned to evacuate their citizens and diplomatic personnel from the area, mainly through charter flights from the country of origin, with the authorization of the Chinese authorities.
Canada, Japan, India, Sri Lanka, Australia, France, France, Argentina, Germany and Thailand were some of the first countries to plan to evacuate their citizens.
Pakistan has said it won't evacuate any citizens from China.
On February 7, Brazil evacuated 34 Brazilians or relatives, in addition to four Poles, one Chinese and a citizen from India.
The citizens of Poland, China and India disembarked in Poland, where the Brazilian jet made a halt before continuing its route to Brazil.
The Brazilian citizens who went to Wuhan were quarantined at a military base near Brasilia.
On the same day, 215 Canadians were evacuated (1-76 from the first plane and 39 from a second U.S. government-tipped plane) from Wuhan to CFB Trenton to be quarantined for two weeks.
On February 11, he landed on CFB Trenton another plane with 185 Canadians who were in Wuhan.
On February 3 and 4, Australian authorities evacuated 277 citizens to the detention centre on Christmas Island, which had been refitted as a quarantine facility, where they stayed for 14 days.
On February 5, an evacuation flight from New Zealand arrived in Auckland; its passengers (including some from Australia and the Pacific) were quarantined at a naval base in Whangaparoa, north of Auckland.
On February 15, the U.S. announced that it would evacuate the United States aboard the Diamond Princess cruise.
On February 21, he landed in Trenton, Ontario, a plane carrying 129 passengers who had been evacuated from the Diamond Princess.
At the beginning of March, the Government of India began to evacuate its citizens from Iran. On March 14, a South African Airways aircraft chartered by the Government of South Africa repatriated 112 South African citizens.
Medical tests have been carried out before take-off, and four South Africans who had signs of coronavirus had to stay to mitigate the risk.
Only the South Africans were repatriated who gave negatives in the tests.
The results of the test authorized all South Africans, including flight crews, pilots, hotel personnel, police and soldiers involved in the humanitarian mission, who, as a pre-preventive measure, remained under observation and quarantine for a period of 14 days at The Ranch Resort.
On March 20, the U.S. began partially removing its troops from Iraq because of the pandemic.
On February 5, the Chinese Foreign Ministry stated that 21 countries (including Belarus, Pakistan, Trinidad and Tobago, Egypt and Iran) had sent aid to China.
Some Chinese students from U.S. universities came together to help send aid to China's regions affected by the virus and, according to reports, a joint group in the Chicago metropolitan area managed to send 50,000 N95 masks to hospitals in Hubei province on January 30. The humanitarian aid organization Direct Relief, in coordination with FedEx on January 30, sent 200,000 masks, in addition to other personal protective equipment, such as gloves and robberies, through emergency air transport to Wuhan Union Hospital.
On February 5, Bill and Melinda Gates announced a $100 million donation to the WHO to fund efforts to search for a vaccine and treatment, in addition to protecting "at-threach populations in South Africa and South Asia."
Interaksyon reported that the Chinese government donated 200,000 masquerades to the Philippines on February 6, after Senator Richard Gordon shipped 3.16 million masks to Wuhan.
On February 19, the Red Cross of Singapore announced that it would send aid to China worth $2.26 million.
Japan donated one million masks to Wuhan, Turkey sent medical equipment, Russia sent more than 13 tons of medical supplies to Wuhan, Malaysia announced a donation of 18 million medical gloves to China, Germany sent several medical supplies, including 10,000 suits for hazardous materials, and the U.S. donated 17.8 tons of medical supplies to China and pledged an additional $100 million in economic aid to the affected countries. After cases in China appeared to stabilize, the country has been sending aid to several countries affected by the pandemic.
In March, China, Cuba and Russia dispatched medical supplies and pundits to help Italy cope with its coronavirus outbreak.
Entre entrepreneur Jack Ma sent 1.1 million test kits, 6 million masks and 60,000 protective suits in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, for distribution by the African Union.
He then sent 5,000 test kits, 100,000 masks and 5 respirators to Panama.
Ma also donated medical supplies to Canada. The Netherlands, Spain, Turkey, Georgia and the Czech Republic expressed their concerns about China-produced masks and test kits.
For example, Spain removed 58,000 coronavirus test kits manufactured in China with an accuracy rate of just 30 per cent, meanwhile the Netherlands removed 600,000 defective Chinese masks.
Belgium removed 100,000 unusable masqueries, which were thought to be from China, but were actually from Colombia.
On the other hand, Chinese assistance was widely accepted in parts of Latin America and Africa. On 2 April, the World Bank launched emergency support operations for developing countries.
WHO has praised the efforts of the Chinese authorities to control and contain the epidemic.
The WHO pointed to the difference between the SARS outbreak of 2002-2004, when the Chinese authorities were accused of secrecy that prevented prevention and containment efforts, and the current crisis, in which the central government "has provided regular updates to prevent panic at the Lunar New Year holidays."
On 23 January, in response to the decision of the central authorities to implement a ban on transportation in Wuhan, WHO representative Gauden Galea noted that, although "it was definitely not a recommendation made by the WHO," it was "a very important indication of the commitment to contain the epidemic at the place where it is most concentrated" and said that it "was unprecedented in the history of public health." On January 30, following the confirmation of transmission between human beings outside China and an increase in the number of cases in other countries, the WHO declared the outbreak a public health emergency of international importance (ESPII), the sixth ESPII since the measure was first invoked during the swine flu pandemic of 2009.
WHO director-general Tedros Adhanom said ESPII owed "the risk of global propagation, especially low- and middle-income countries without robust health systems."
In response to the implementation of the travel restrictions, Tedros stated that "there is no reason to take unnecessarily measures in international trade and travel" and that "WHO does not recommend limiting trade or movement."
On February 5, the WHO resorted to the global community for a contribution of $675 million to finance strategic preparedness in low-income countries, citing the urgency of supporting those countries "that do not have systems to detect the people who contracted the virus, if any."
Tedros also made statements in which he stated that "we are only as strong as our weakest link" and urged the international community to "reverse today or pay more in the future." On February 11, the WHO announced at a press conference that COVID-19 was the name of the disease.
On the same day, Tedros stated that the Secretary-General of the United Nations, António Guterres, had Agreed to provide "power of the entire United Nations system in the response."
As a result, a United Nations crisis management team was activated, which enabled the coordination of the United Nations-wide response, which WHO says will enable them to "focus on the health response while the other agencies can contribute their expertise to address the broader social, economic and development implications of the outbreak."
On February 14, a WHO-led joint mission team was activated with China to provide international and WHO experts on the ground in China to assist in national management and to assess "the severity and transmissibility of the disease" through organizing workshops and meetings with major institutions at the country level and to conduct site visits to assess the "imbursement impact of response activities at the provincial and county levels, including the urban and rural environments." On February 25, WHO stated that "the world must do more to prepare for a possible coronavirus pandemic," and noted that, although it was still too early to call it pandemic, countries should nevertheless be "in a preparatory phase."
In response to an ongoing outbreak in Iran, the WHO dispatched a joint mission team there to assess the situation. On 28 February, WHO officials said the global coronavirus threat assessment would rise from "high" to "very high," its highest level of warning and risk assessment.
Mike Ryan, executive director of WHO's health emergencies program, warned in a statement that "This is a verification of the reality for all governments on the planet.
"This virus can be on the way and we must be prepared," insisting that the right response measures could help the world avoid "the worst."
Ryan also said the current data didn't guarantee that public health officials would declare a global pandemic, noting that such a statement would mean "basically we are agreeing that all human beings on the planet will be exposed to that virus."
On March 11, the WHO declared the coronavirus outbreak pandemic.
The director-general said WHO was "very concerned, both with the alarming levels of spread and severity, and the alarming levels of inaction." The WHO has received significant criticism of what is seen as inadequate management of the pandemic, including the belated declaration of a public health emergency and the classification of the virus as a pandemic.
Negative backlash included the request for the resignation of WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom, signed by 733 000 people as of April 6.
On March 26, 2020, dozens of United Nations human rights experts emphasized respecting the rights of all people during the COVID-19 pandemic.
The expert group indicated that everyone has the right to life-saving interventions and that the Government bears this responsibility.
The group emphasized that the lack of resources or health insurance should never be used as a justification for discrimination against a specific group.
The experts emphasized that everyone has the right to health, including persons with disabilities, those belonging to minority groups, the elderly, internally displaced persons, the homeless, those living in extreme poverty, detained persons, as well as refugees and other unspecified groups in need of government support.
International governmental organizations are addressing the social and economic impacts of the COVID-19 crisis.
&quot; The Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development launched a platform to provide comprehensive and timely information on policy responses in countries around the world, in addition to views and advice.
From policies aimed at strengthening health systems and the global economy to addressing the effects of confinement and travel restrictions, the digital center includes a policy tracker by country, and aims to help countries learn from each other and to facilitate a coordinated global response to the challenge of Coronavirus.
The Government of China has been criticized by the United States, the Minister of the United Kingdom Cabinet Office, Michael Gove, and Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro &apos; s son, Eduardo Bolsonaro, for his handling of the pandemic, which began in the Chinese province of Hubei.
A number of administrators at the provincial level of the Chinese Communist Party (CPC) have been sacked because of their handling of quarantine efforts in central China, a sign of discontent with the political system's response to the outbreak in those regions.
Some commentators believe that this measure was taken to protect Chinese Communist Party Secretary General Xi Jinping from public anger over the coronavirus outbreak.
Some Chinese officials, for example, Zhao Lijian, dismissed an earlier acknowledgment at the start of the Coronavirus outbreak in Wuhan, in favor of conspiratory theories about the origin of COVID-19 in the U.S. or Italy.
Donald Trump's U.S. administration has referred to Coronavirus as the "Chinese virus" or " Wuhan's "virus," and stated that China's "censorship has overpowered a virus that has now become a global pandemic," which, in turn, was pointed out by some critics as racism and "discussion of the failure of its administration to contain the disease."
The Daily Beast obtained a U.S. government telegram describing a ploy of communications with an apparent departure from the National Security Council, citing "Everything about China."
We have been told to try to convey this message in every way possible, including press conferences and television appearances. "Medicines such as Politico, Foreign Policy and Bloomberg have stated that China's efforts to send aid to the countries affected by the virus are part of propaganda to gain global influence.
The foreign policy representative of the European Union, Josep Borrell, warned that "there is a geopolitical component that includes a struggle for influence through propaganda and the policy of generosity."
Borrell also said that "China is aggressively pushing the message that, unlike the United States, it is a responsible and reliable partner."
China also called for the U.S. to lift its sanctions against Syria, Venezuela and Iran, while it reportedly sent aid to the latter two countries.
U.S. sanctions on April 3 blocked Jack Ma's donation of 100,000 masks to Cuba.
U.S. authorities have also been accused of diverting aid to other countries to their own country.
There have been reports of disputes related to the masks among other countries, such as Germany, Austria and Switzerland; and between the Czech Republic and Italy.
In addition, Turkey seized hundreds of respirators destined for Spain.
At the beginning of March, the Italian government criticised the European Union &apos; s lack of solidarity with Italy affected by Coronavirus.
Maurizio Massari, Italy &apos; s ambassador to the European Union, said: "Only China responded bilaterally.
This is certainly not a good sign of European solidarity."
On March 22, after a telephone call with Italian Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte, Russian President Vladimir Putin ordered the Russian army to send military doctors, special disenfection vehicles and other medical equipment to Italy.
Italy's newspaper La Stampa cited an anonymous "high-level political source" who claimed that 80 per cent of Russia's aid was "useless or unused for Italy."
The source accused Russia of launching an "geopolitical and diplomatic seduction offensive."
Lombardy President Attilio Fontana and Italian Foreign Minister Luigi Di Maio dismissed the newspaper reports and expressed their gratitude.
Russia also shipped a cargo jet with medical help to the United States.
Kremlin spokeswoman Dmitry Peskov said that "by offering aid to U.S. colleagues [Putin] assumes that, when U.S. manufacturers of materials and medical equipment gain momentum, they will also be able to reciprocate if necessary."
NATO &apos; s planned "Defender 2020" military exercise in Germany, Poland and the Baltic States, NATO's largest war exercise since the end of the Cold War, will take place on a reduced scale.
The Secretary-General of the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament, Kate Hudson, criticised the Defender 2020 exercise: "In the current public health crisis, it threatens the lives not only of U.S. troops and the various participating European countries, but also those of the inhabitants of the countries in which they operate."
On March 14, 2020, the president of Iran, Hassan Rouhani, wrote a public letter to world leaders asking for help, saying that his country is struggling to combat the outbreak due to a lack of access to international markets as a result of U.S. U.S. sanctions against Iran. The outbreak fueled the requirement that the U.S. adopt common social policies in other wealthy countries, such as universal health care, universal child care, paid family leave and increased levels of public health funds.
Politician analysts anticipated that it could negatively affect Donald Trump's re-election chances in the 2020 presidential election.Dimmary relations between Japan and South Korea worsened due to the pandemic.
South Korea criticised Japan's "ambitious and passive quarantine efforts," after Japan announced that anyone from South Korea would be quarantined for two weeks at government-appointed sites.
South Korea's society at first was polarized about Moon Jae-in's response to the crisis.
Many North Koreans signed petitions demanding Moon's ouster for what they saw as mishandling the outbreak by the government, or praising its response. The pandemic has allowed countries to pass emergency legislation in response.
Some commentators have expressed concern that it could allow governments to strengthen their control of power.
In Hungary, its Parliament voted to allow Prime Minister Viktor Orbán to rule by decree indefinitely, to suspend Parliament and the elections and punish those deemed to have disseminated false information about the virus and the government's handling of the crisis.
The coronavirus bout was blamed for a number of shortages of supplies as a result of the world's increased use of buds-flying equipment, panic-driven purchases and interruptions of logistics and factory operations.
The U.S. U.S. Food and Drug Administration has issued warnings about a shortage of medicines and medical equipment due to increased demand from consumers and disruption to suppliers.
Several localities also witnessed panic-fuelled purchases that left the shelves empty of essential products, such as food, toilet paper and bottled water, resulting in a shortage of supplies.
The tech sector, in particular, has been reversing delays in shipments of e-products.
According to WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom, the demand for personal protective equipment increased by 100 times.
This demand generated an increase in prices of up to 20 times the normal price and it also caused delays of four to six months in the supply of medical items.
It also led to a shortage of personal protective equipment around the world, and the WHO warned that this would endanger health workers.
In Australia, the pandemic provided a new opportunity for Daligou buyers to sell Australian products in China.
The activity has created a shortage of formula for babies in some supermarkets and was subsequently banned by the Australian Government. Despite the high prevalence of COVID-19 cases in northern Italy and the Wuhan region, and the ensuing demand for food, none of these areas were affected by acute food shortages.
China and Italy &apos; s measures against the accumulation and illicit trade of essential products have been successful, preventing an acute shortage of food anticipated in Europe and in North America.
The north of Italy, with its significant agricultural production, has not had a significant reduction, but prices could increase, according to representatives of the sector.
Food shelves were only temporarily emptied, including in the city of Wuhan, while Chinese government officials released pork reserves to ensure sufficient food from the population.
There are similar laws in Italy that require food producers to maintain reserves for these emergencies.
The damage to the global economy was felt in China, according to a March 16 newspaper report, China's economy was badly damaged in the first two months of 2020 due to measures taken by the government to stop the spread of the virus, and retail sales fell by 20.5 per cent.
Since mainland China is a major economic and manufacturing hub, the viral outbreak has been seen as a considerable destabilization threat to the global economy.
Agathe Demarais of The Economist's Intelligence Unit has predicted that markets will remain volatile until a clearer picture emerges about the potential consequences.
In January 2020, some Analysts estimated that the economic consequences of the epidemic on global growth could outweigh those of the SARS outbreak of 2002-2004.
An espiator's estimate at the University of Washington in St. Louis indicated an impact of more than $300-billion on the world's supply chains that could last up to two years.
The Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was reportedly "revolved" after a sharp drop in oil prices due to lower demand from China.
On February 24, global stock markets fell due to a significant increase in COVID-19 cases outside mainland China.
On February 27, due to growing worries about the coronavirus outbreak, several U.S. stock indices, including the NASDAQ-100, the S&P 500 index and the Dow Jones Industrial Average posted its steepest falls since 2008, the Dow dropped 1191 points, the biggest one-day fall since the 2007-08 financial crisis.
The three indexes ended the week with losses of more than 10 per cent.
On February 28, Scope Ratings GmbH affirmed China's sovereign credit rating but maintained a negative outlook.
Shares fell again based on wreath-living fears, the biggest fall on March 16.
Many believe that there is likely to be an economic downturn.
Economist Mohamed El-Erian praised the appropriate emergency measures of states and central banks.
Central banks are reacting faster than they reacted to the 2008 financial collapse.
Tourism is one of the sectors most affected because of travel bans, the closure of public places that include travel attractions and the recommendation of governments not to travel to any part of the world.
As a result, many airlines have cancelled flights due to lower demand, including British Airways, China Eastern Airlines and Qantas, while British regional airline Flybe collapsed.
The impact of cruise companies has reached a never-before-seen level.
Several railway stations and ferry terminals have also been closed.
The epidemic coincided with Chunyun, an important travel season related to China's New Year's Eve.
A number of events involving large crowds were cancelled by national and regional governments, including New Year's Eve festivals, while private companies also independently closed their shops and tourist attractions such as Hong Kong Disneyland and Shanghai Disneyland.
Many events and tourist attractions of the Lunar New Year have been closed to avoid mass rallies, including the Forbidden City of Beijing and traditional temple fairs.
In 24 of China's 31 provinces, municipalities and regions, authorities extended New Year's Eve until February 10, and ordered most workplaces not to reopen until that date.
These regions represent 80 per cent per cent of GDP and 90 per cent of the country &apos; s exports.
Hong Kong elevated its level of response to the infectious disease at its highest and declared an emergency, closed schools until March and cancelled its New Year's Eve Celebrations. The retail sector was affected globally, with reductions in care hours or temporary closures.
Visits to retailers in Europe and Latin America decreased by 40 per cent.
Re retailers in North America and the Middle East experienced a 50 per cent drop to 60 per cent.
This also resulted in a 33 to 43 per cent drop in pedestrian transit to malls in March compared to Feb.
Shopping mall operators around the world imposed additional measures, such as greater hygiene, the installation of thermal scanners to control the temperature of the buyers and cancellation of events. According to an estimate by the United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America, the pandemic-induced recession could leave between 14 and 22 million more people in a situation of extreme poverty in Latin America than had been left without the pandemic.
In January and February 2020, at the height of the epidemic in Wuhan, about 5 million people in China lost their jobs.
Many of China's nearly 300 million rural migrant workers were stranded in their homes in inner provinces or trapped in Hubei Province. In March 2020, more than 10 million Americans lost their jobs and requested government help.
The kronoty outbreak could cost 47 million jobs in the U.S. and the unemployment rate can reach 32 percent, according to estimates by the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis. Confinement in India has left tens of millions of Indian migrant workers (receiving daily wages) unemployed. According to the Angus Reid Institute survey, 44 percent of Canadian households experienced some form of unemployment.About 900 thousand workers lost their jobs in Spain since confinement began in mid-March 2020.
During the second half of March, 4 million French workers applied for temporary unemployment benefits and 1 million British workers applied for a universal credit plan. Almost half a million companies in Germany have implemented short-term work schemes subsidized by the government, known as Kurzarbeit, for their workers.
Germany's short-term labour compensation scheme has been adopted by France and Britain.
Performing sectors and cultural heritage were profoundly affected by the pandemic, which had an impact on the operations of the organizations and on individuals, both employed and independent, around the world.
Organizations in the culture and the arts sector tried to carry out their mission (often publicly funded) to provide the community with access to cultural heritage, to maintain the security of their employees and the public and to support artists where possible.
By March 2020, around the world and to varying degrees, museums, libraries, entertainment venues and other cultural institutions had closed indefinitely, and their exhibitions, events and spectacles were cancelled or postponed.
In response, great efforts have been made to provide alternative services through digital platforms. Another recent consequence that increases at an accelerated rate of the disease is the cancellation of religious services. important sporting events and other social events, such as music festivals and concerts, technology conferences and fashion parades.
The film industry also suffered interruptions. The Vatican has announced the cancellation of the Holy Week celebrations in Rome, which take place during the last week of Lent Christian penance season.
Many dioceses have recommended that older Christians stay in their homes rather than participate in Mass on Sundays; some churches offered religious services through radio, live broadcast on the Internet or television, while others offer space to pray from the car.
Following the decision of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Rome to close its churches and chapels and with St.Peter's Square empty of Christian pilgrims, other religious organizations also cancelled their services and limited public rallies in churches, mosques, synagogues, temples and gurdwaras.
Iran's Ministry of Health Announced Friday Prayers in Areas Affected by the R&S and Sanctuaries were then closed, while Saudi Arabia barred foreign pilgrims and its residents from entering holy sites in Mecca and Medina.
The pandemic has provoked the most significant altercation on the global sports calendar since World War II.
Most of the major sporting events were canceled or postponed, among them the 2019-20 UEFA Champions Champions League, the 2019-20 Premier League, UEFA Euro 2020, the 2019-20 NBA season and NHL's 2019-20 season.
The outbreak disrupted plans for the 2020 Olympic Games, which were originally scheduled to begin at the end of July; the International Olympic Committee announced on March 24 that the event will "reschedule for a date after 2020 but not after the summer of 2021." Casinos and other gambling venues around the world closed and online poker tournaments were postponed or canceled.
This caused many gamblers to turn to the Internet, many online gambling sites reported significant increases in their rates of new enrollment. The entertainment industry was also affected, several music groups suspended or cancelled concert tours.
Many large theaters, such as those on Broadway, also suspended all the shows.
Some artists have scouted ways to keep producing and share their work across the internet as an alternative to the traditional live spectacle, such as live concert broadcasts or the creation of "festivals" on the Internet for artists to interpret, distribute or publish their work.
Online, numerous Internet memes were disseminated about coronavirus, with many turning to humor and distraction amid uncertainty.
Since the COVID-19 outbreak, there has been an increase in prejudices, xenophobia and racism towards people of Chinese and East Asian descent, and to people from areas of Europe, the United States and other countries where the situation is critical.
There have been incidents of fear, suspicion and hostility in many countries, particularly in Europe, East Asia, North America and the Asia-Pacific region.
February reports (when most cases were still limited to China) have documented racist feelings in several groups around the world expressing that the Chinese deserved the virus or were receiving what they considered a fair reprisal.
Some countries in Africa have also seen an increase in anti-Chinese sentiment.
Many residents of Wuhan and Hubei have reported discrimination on the basis of their regional origin.
The Chinese and those in the areas affected by the virus have received support, both on and off the Internet.
Following the advance of the outbreak towards new countries where the situation is critical, people in Italy, the first country in Europe to experience a serious outbreak of COVID-19, could also be the subject of suspicion and xenophobia. The citizens of countries such as Malaysia, New Zealand, Singapore and South Korea initially signed petitions for a ban on Chinese entry into their countries in an effort to stop the disease.
In Japan the hashtag "Chinese DontComeToJapan" was trending on Twitter.
The Chinese, in addition to other Asian people in the United Kingdom and the United States, have reported higher levels of racist slurs, in addition to attacks.
U.S. President Donald Trump was criticised for referring to Coronavirus as "Chinese virus," a term many critics deemed racist and anti-Chinese.
Demonstrators in Ukraine attacked buses carrying Ukrainian and foreign evacuees from Wuhan to Novi Sanzhary.
Students from north-eastern India, who share a border with China and studying in major Indian cities, were reportedly harassed in connection with the coronavirus outbreak.
The president of the state unit of the Bharatiya Janata Party in West Bengal, Dilip Ghosh, said that the Chinese had destroyed nature and "that's why God took revenge on them."
China's consulate in Calcutta then condemned the comments, saying they were "wrong." In China, xenophobia and racism towards non-Chinese residents increased as a result of the pandemic, and foreigners were described as "foreign garbage" and destined for "disdating."
Lots of pay-per-fills newspapers removed them for parts or all of their coronavirus coverage.
Scientological publishers published scientific articles related to the unexpended outbreak with open access.
Some scientists chose to quickly share their results on pre-impression servers such as bioRxiv.
