Severe Serious Accrustrast Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) coronavirus is a new coronavirus for severe acute respiratory syndrome, which was first isolated from three people with pneumonia connected to a group of cases of acute respiratory disease in Wuhan.
All characteristics of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus can be found in coronaviruses of a related nature.
Outside the human body, domestic soap kills the virus as it bursts its protective bubble. SARS-CoV-2 is closely linked to the original SARS-CoV.
The lungs are the organs most affected by COVID-19, because the virus enters the host cells using the angiotensin Type 2 converting enzyme (ECA2), which accompanies more in type II alveolar cells of the lungs.
The virus uses a special surface glycoprotein called "pppprone" (peplómer) to connect with ACE2 and into the host cell.
An acute cardiac lesion was identified for 12 per cent of infected persons hospitalized in Wuhan, China, which is most common in severe disease.
