WHO has published several test protocols for detecting the disease.
The standard method of testing is polymerase chain reaction with real-time reverse transcribing (rRT-PCR).
The test is usually carried out in samples of the airways obtained by a nasopharyngeal swindle; however, samples of nasal swindles or shoes may also be used.
Usually, the results are available between a few hours or two days.
Blood tests can also be used, but two blood samples should be taken two weeks apart and the results have very little immediate value.
Chinese scientists managed to insulate the coronavirus strain and published the genetic sequeetition so the world's labs could independently develop polymerase-chain (PCR) reaction tests to detect infection with the virus.
As of April 4, 2020, antibody tests (which can detect active infections and if the person was infected in the past) were being developed, but were not widely used.
Experience in China with the tests has shown that accuracy is only 60 to 70 per cent.
The FDA in the U.S. approved the first immediate diagnostic test on March 21, 2020 for use at the end of that month. Diagnostic guidelines published by Wuhan University's Zhongnan Hospital suggested methods for detecting infections based on clinical characteristics and epidemiological risk.
