The main pathological results of the autopsies are as follows:
Macroscopy: pleuritis, pericarditis, lung consolidation and lung oedema
You can observe four types of gravity of viral pneumonia:
Mild pneumonia: pulmonar lung oedema, pneumocyte hyperplasia, atypically large pneumocytes, interstitial inflammation with lymphocyte infiltration and the formation of gigantic cells with multiple nuclei
Severe pneumonia: diffuse alveolar damage (DAD) with diffuse alvelolar exudates.
DAD is the cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome (RASD) and severe hypoxemia.
pneumonia in healing: organization of exudates in alvelolar cavities and lung interstitial fibrosis
Blose: disseminated intravascular coagulation (CID); leucoeritroblastic reaction
Premeditative measures to reduce the chances of infection include staying in the house, avoiding places with many people, washing your hands with soap water often for at least 20 seconds, having good respiratory hygiene and avoiding touching your eyes, nose or mouth without washing your hands.
The CDC recommends covering your mouth and nose with a handkerchief when coughing or sneezing and, if you don't have handkerchiefs, wear the inner elbow.
Proceed hand hygiene is recommended after coughing or sneezing.
The CDC has recommended the use of cloth covers in public places, one of the reasons is to limit the transmission by asymptomatic people. Social estrangement strategies seek to reduce the contact of people infected with large groups by closing schools and workplaces, restricting travel and canceling crowded public meetings.
Deviation patterns also indicate that people should maintain a distance of 6 feet (1.8 m).
There's not an effective medicine to prevent COVID-19. Since there is not expected to be a vaccine until 2021, at the very earliest, a key part of controlling COVID-19 consists of trying to prevent epidemic spike, what's known as "flashing the curve."
The CDCs also recommend washing your hands frequently with soap and soap water for at least 20 seconds, particularly after going to the bathroom or when your hands are visibly dirty, before eating and smiling your nose, coughing or sneezing.
They also recommend the use of an alcohol-based hand sanitizer with at least 60 per cent of alcohol, but only when soap and soap aren't water available in a timely manner. For areas where hand disinfectants aren't easy to get, WHO offers two formulations for local production.
In these formulations, anti-microbial activity results from ethanol or isopropanol.
