The World Health Organization announced on February 11, 2020 that the official name for the disease would be "COVID-19."
WHO chief Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus explained that CO refers to corona, VI to viruses, D to disease and 19 to the date the outbreak was discovered: December 31, 2019.
The name was chosen to avoid the references to a specific geographical location (e.g., China), an animal species or a group of people, in line with the international recommendations called to prevent stigmatization. Virus that CausuaD-19 Causes COVID-19 is called coronavirus of severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
The WHO also uses "the COVID-19 virus" and "the Virus responsible for COVID-19" in public communications.
It is common to use "coronaviruses" to refer to both the disease and the virus.
