There's no drugs or vaccines approved to treat the disease.
Governmental organizations, academic groups and industry researchers are carrying out international research on Vaccines and Messages for COVID-19.
In March, the World Health Organization launched the " SOLIDARITY Essay" to evaluate the therapeutic effects of the four most promising existing antiviral compounds in terms of effectiveness.
There's no vaccine, but several agencies are actively developing possible vaccines.
Previous work on SARS-CoV is being used because SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 use ACE2-receiving ACE to enter human cells.
Three vaccination strategies are being investigated.
First, the researchers are looking to develop a complete virus vaccine.
The use of this virus, whether inactive or dead, is intended to generate a rapid immune response from the human body to a new anticOVID-19 virus infection.
A second strategy, sub-unfacing vaccines, seeks to create a vaccine that sensitizes the immune system to certain subunits of the virus.
For SARS-CoV-2, such research is focused on protein S that helps the virus to get into receptor ACE2.
A third strategy is nucleic acid vaccines (DNA or RNA vaccines, a new technique for creating vaccines).
Experimental vaccines from any of these strategies should be studied to determine their safety and efficacy. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of a four-volunteer-to-ceiling vaccine began in Seattle.
The vaccine contains an innocuous copy of the genetic code of the virus that causes the disease. It has been suggested that anti-anti-anti-boosting improvement is a potential challenge for the development of SARS-COV-2 vaccines, but this is controversial.
By April 2020 there are more than 300 active clinical trials.
Seven trials evaluated already approved treatments for malaria, including four hydroxy chloroquine or chloroquine studies.
Readapted anti-viral drugs make up the bulk of China's research, including nine Phase III remakes on remdesivir in a number of countries that are due to report by the end of April.
As of April 2020, a dynamic review of clinical developments was under way on the vaccine and possible COVID-19 drugs. A number of anti-vivage medicinal products are evaluated for the treatment of COVID-19, including: remdesivir,  Chloquine and hydroxychloroquin, lopinavir/ritonavir and lopinavir/ritonavir in combination with interferon-beta.
